LIVRO = 'Veganismo Baseado em Evidências (Evidence-Based Veganism, in Portuguese)'
INCERTEZA = '''<div class="bloco curta"><p><strong>How to decide under uncertainty.</strong> Instead of asking only "is this being sentient, yes or no?", ask how likely it is and weigh that by the size of the harm or benefit at stake. A moderate probability multiplied by an immense scale can outweigh a high certainty over small numbers. Uncertainty about a borderline case does not contaminate the certainty about the criterion: sentience is still what matters.</p></div>'''

ALEM = {
 'antiespecismo': dict(s='antispeciesism', selo='Foundation', t='Antispeciesism and sentientism',
  res='Veganism is one consequence of a broader criterion: what matters morally is the capacity to be harmed or benefited.',
  corpo='''<h2>Two names, one idea</h2><p><strong>Speciesism</strong>, as the philosopher Oscar Horta defines it, is the unjustified disadvantageous consideration or treatment of those who are not classified as belonging to a certain species (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10806-009-9205-2">What Is Speciesism?</a>). The definition covers placing animals below humans and also placing some species below others, such as treating pigs worse than dogs. <strong>Anthropocentrism</strong>, which favors humans, is the most common form of speciesism, but not the only one. And a justified difference in treatment is not speciesism, just as it is not sexism that only women receive gynecological care.</p><p><strong>Antispeciesism</strong> rejects that discrimination: it gives similar weight to similar harms, whatever the species. It follows from <strong>sentientism</strong>, the criterion that what matters morally is who can be harmed or benefited, explained below.</p><p>That is why the discussion does not end at the plate. If we accept the criterion, it also applies to what we do by omission, to animals no one exploits and to beings that may come to exist.</p>
<h2>Sentientism: what it is and what it is not</h2>
<p><strong>Sentientism</strong> (also called <strong>sentiocentrism</strong>) is the position that what matters morally is the capacity to have positive and negative experiences. A being that can be harmed or benefited from the point of view of the one living the situation has interests of its own, and those interests count in themselves, not because of what others do with the being. Jeremy Bentham's question, "can they suffer?", is usually cited as the origin of the idea (<a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</a>). The term was already in use in 1977, in a text by John Rodman (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentientism">Wikipedia</a>).</p>
<h3>Old ideas, new name</h3>
<p>Ideas close to sentientism are much older than the term. In <strong>Jainism</strong>, <em>ahimsa</em>, non-violence, applies to all living beings, and classical texts such as the <em>Tattvartha Sutra</em> classify beings by the number of senses they have, from touch, in one-sensed beings, up to the five senses of animals and humans. In <strong>Buddhism</strong>, the first precept asks people not to take life, and compassion is directed at all sentient beings. Hindu traditions also extend <em>ahimsa</em> to animals and link it to vegetarianism. In ancient Greece, the <strong>Pythagoreans</strong> and <strong>Empedocles</strong> condemned killing and eating animals; <strong>Theophrastus</strong>, Aristotle's successor, argued against animal sacrifice by appealing to the kinship between animals and humans; <strong>Plutarch</strong> wrote <em>On the Eating of Flesh</em>; and <strong>Porphyry</strong>, in the third century, gathered many of these arguments in <em>On Abstinence</em>, which preserved passages from Theophrastus's lost book. Much later, in 1789, Bentham summed up the criterion in his question about the capacity to suffer.</p>
<p>These traditions were not sentientism in the modern sense. Many rested on rebirth or the transmigration of souls, on the purity of the one who acts or on spiritual progress, and not only on the interest of the animal itself. Jainism extends <em>ahimsa</em> to one-sensed beings, such as plants, and even to earth and water, which are not considered sentient today. And many of these societies, Buddhist and Hindu ones included, accepted eating meat and other uses of animals. Even so, they show that the idea that animal suffering matters in itself is not a recent invention.</p>
<p><strong>Sentience is not only pain.</strong> It covers anything that can be lived as good or bad: pain, fear, frustration, boredom, comfort, pleasure, play, attachment. So the criterion includes positive well-being, not only the absence of suffering. The scientific declarations cited below speak of consciousness, and here are read as evidence about sentience.</p>
<h3>Criterion, consequence and practice</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Sentientism</strong> is the criterion: who is inside the moral circle.</li><li><strong>Antispeciesism</strong> is a consequence: if what counts is sentience, species cannot be the factor that decides whether an interest weighs or not.</li><li><strong>Veganism</strong> is a practice that follows for the use of animals. Sentientism goes further: it also applies to animals no one exploits, to invertebrates and, if it comes to that, to digital minds.</li></ul>
<p>The criterion is defended, with important differences among them, by <strong>Peter Singer</strong> (for whom the capacity to suffer is a prerequisite for having interests: it makes no sense to say that kicking a stone goes against its interests, but a mouse does have an interest in not being mistreated), <strong>Oscar Horta</strong> and <strong>Luciano Carlos Cunha</strong>, and the <strong>Sentientism</strong> movement, developed by <strong>Jamie Woodhouse</strong>, which describes it as a worldview built on evidence, reason and compassion for all sentient beings (<a href="https://sentientism.info/">sentientism.info</a>). <strong>Jeff Sebo</strong> argues for widening the moral circle to beings with a non-negligible chance of well-being, including invertebrates and AI, but he combines sentience with other possible bases, such as agency. <strong>Jonathan Birch</strong> works on the practical problem of acting without knowing who feels (see below). Other names help develop the criterion: <strong>Richard Ryder</strong>, who coined the term "speciesism" in 1970 and proposed painism (<em>Painism</em>, 2003); <strong>Alasdair Cochrane</strong>, who proposes a sentientist political theory (<em>Sentientist Politics</em>, 2018); <strong>Catia Faria</strong>, who addresses wild animals (<em>Animal Ethics in the Wild</em>, 2022); <strong>Magnus Vinding</strong>, who defends suffering-focused ethics (<em>Suffering-Focused Ethics</em>, 2020); and <strong>Jacy Reese Anthis</strong>, who studies moral circle expansion, including to digital minds. In science, researchers such as <strong>Robert Elwood</strong>, who studied pain in crustaceans, and the philosopher <strong>Kristin Andrews</strong>, one of the organizers of the New York Declaration (2024), help show who feels. The philosopher <strong>David Pearce</strong>, in <em>The Antispeciesist Revolution</em> (2013), criticizes the idea that consciousness works like a dimmer switch tied to intelligence: the most intense experiences, such as agony and terror, depend on the limbic system, which humans share with other vertebrates. Not every defender accepts every conclusion of the others.</p>

<h2>The variants of sentientism</h2>
<p>Accepting that sentience matters leaves two questions open. The criteria table on the site <em>Ética Para Iniciantes</em> (Ethics for Beginners, in Portuguese) calls the answers partial versions of sentientism, as opposed to the full version.</p>
<h3>Gradualist or non-gradualist</h3>
<p>The question: <strong>does an experience of the same intensity count equally in any being?</strong></p>
<ul><li>The <strong>non-gradualist</strong> says yes: being sentient is enough, and an equal pain weighs the same, whether in a human or a pig.</li><li>The <strong>gradualist</strong> says the weight varies with capacities such as intelligence and self-awareness: more complex minds would have interests of greater weight. There is also a reading in which the weight varies with the degree or richness of sentience itself.</li></ul>
<p>One distinction keeps the positions from being confused. <strong>What is at stake</strong> changes with capacities: a being with plans and an anticipated future loses more by dying than one without, and almost no one disputes that. <strong>The weight of an equal harm</strong> is the gradualism question proper.</p>
<p><strong>Who holds what.</strong> Singer defends the principle of equal consideration of interests, which is not equal treatment nor equal weight for everything (see <a href="../argumentos/n20.html">N20</a>). Shelly Kagan, in <em>How to Count Animals, More or Less</em> (2019), defends a hierarchy: persons count more than animals and some animals count more than others. David DeGrazia criticized Kagan's claim that humans lacking the capacities of persons get extra status.</p>
<p><strong>Main objections.</strong> (1) The argument from marginal cases: if intelligence sets the weight, infants and people with severe cognitive disability would weigh less, and many adult animals would weigh more than they do. Gradualist replies appeal to potential, to relationships or to Kagan's "modal personhood", and each is contested. (2) The intensity of pain may not track intelligence: a simple mind, with no consolation or distraction, may suffer a great deal.</p>
<h3>Temporalist or non-temporalist</h3>
<p>The question: <strong>does suffering or well-being count for less because it happens later?</strong></p>
<ul><li>The <strong>temporalist</strong> gives less weight to the future, because of uncertainty, distance, or the view that future beings are less real, applying something like a discount rate.</li><li>The <strong>non-temporalist</strong> (atemporalist) says the date does not change the value: like distance in space, it changes what we know, not what it is worth.</li></ul>
<p>Derek Parfit distinguished discounting for <strong>uncertainty</strong>, which is legitimate (we weigh by probability), from <strong>pure</strong> discounting, merely for being later, which he rejected; Henry Sidgwick had already said that time does not change value. Longtermism (Toby Ord, William MacAskill) takes the distant future seriously, with criticisms about fanaticism. Objection: today's decisions change <em>who</em> will exist (Parfit's non-identity problem), which complicates talk of "harming" future beings.</p>
<h3>The four combinations</h3>
<p>The table below is a reading of the logical implications of each combination, not a classification published by any author.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Combination</th><th scope="col">Insects</th><th scope="col">Fetuses</th><th scope="col">Future beings</th><th scope="col">Death</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Non-gradualist and non-temporalist</strong> (full version)</td><td>Count if sentient; an equal experience weighs the same</td><td>Count from the moment there is sentience, an open empirical question</td><td>Count like present beings, discounting only for uncertainty</td><td>Matters for what the being loses, not for its species</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Gradualist and non-temporalist</strong></td><td>Count with a lower individual weight; the huge numbers may compensate (<a href="../argumentos/n20.html">N20</a>)</td><td>Lower weight while capacities are lower</td><td>Count like present beings, weighted by capacities</td><td>Weighs more for more complex minds</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Non-gradualist and temporalist</strong></td><td>As in the full version</td><td>As in the full version</td><td>Count less the further away in time</td><td>Loss of future life weighs less the more distant it is</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Gradualist and temporalist</strong></td><td>Lower individual weight</td><td>Lower weight</td><td>Lower weight twice: by capacity and by distance</td><td>Weighs less, except for complex minds and near lives</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Two caveats. Whether a fetus feels, and when, depends on neuroscience, not on the variant; Birch discusses it among the cases of uncertain sentience. And on death there is a third question, that of the <strong>interest in living</strong>: Elizabeth Harman argues that painlessly killing a healthy animal is a significant harm, against the idea that only pain matters.</p>
<h2>Sentientism and the other criteria</h2>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">What counts</th><th scope="col">Who is left out</th><th scope="col">Main problem</th><th scope="col">Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Anthropocentrism</strong></td><td>Humans</td><td>All nonhumans, or they get only indirect duties</td><td>Species is a biological trait; reason and language are missing in some humans who count and present in some animals who do not (marginal cases)</td><td>Kant, with indirect duties toward animals (SEP)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rationality, autonomy, personhood</strong></td><td>Those who reason and govern themselves</td><td>Infants, people with severe cognitive disability and most animals</td><td>Same marginal-cases difficulty; Tom Regan answers with "subject of a life", which asks for less than rationality</td><td>Kant; Regan</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Social relations and contract</strong></td><td>Those who take part in agreements, bonds or reciprocity</td><td>Those who cannot bargain, reciprocate or have a recognized bond</td><td>Turns bargaining power into value; Mark Rowlands uses the veil of ignorance to include animals</td><td>Contractualism; Rowlands</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Interest in living</strong></td><td>Beings with preferences about the future</td><td>Those who feel but do not plan</td><td>Explains why death matters, not why pain matters; usually added to sentientism, not opposed to it</td><td>Singer; Harman, in a different sense</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Biocentrism</strong></td><td>Every living being</td><td>Only what is not alive; among the living, no one</td><td>Having a good of its own without being able to feel creates no one who cares. Peter Singer notes that saying a plant "seeks" the light is a metaphor that would fit a river "seeking" the sea just as well, and asks why a tree should deserve more respect than a stalactite. Biocentrists reply that having a good of its own is enough</td><td>Schweitzer, Taylor, Attfield</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ecocentrism and holism</strong></td><td>Ecosystems, species, the biosphere</td><td>The individuals inside them, in a conflict</td><td>The whole does not feel. Singer's test is to ask what it would be, for the subject itself, to fail to flourish: the question has an answer for an animal that is drowning, but not for a species or an ecosystem. Regan called putting the whole first "environmental fascism". Callicott and Rolston argue that the whole has value of its own</td><td>Leopold, Callicott, Naess</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sentientism</strong></td><td>Whoever can have good or bad experiences</td><td>Those who are not sentient (if any)</td><td>Depends on knowing who feels; comparing experiences across species is hard</td><td>Singer, Horta, Cunha, Woodhouse</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Possibilities and limits</h2>
<h3>Who is sentient?</h3>
<p>We do not observe another being's experience, only signs in behavior, brain and evolution. Two declarations by researchers help, without closing the question. The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge_Declaration_on_Consciousness">Cambridge Declaration (2012)</a> states that humans are not unique in having the neurological substrates that generate consciousness, and mentions mammals, birds and octopuses. The <a href="https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration">New York Declaration (2024)</a> says there is strong scientific support for mammals and birds and at least a realistic possibility of conscious experience in all vertebrates and many invertebrates (cephalopods, decapod crustaceans and insects), and that ignoring that possibility is irresponsible. Both are statements by experts, not proof.</p>
<p>Under uncertainty, Birch proposes treating as "sentience candidates" the systems with a credible, non-negligible possibility of feeling, and acting proportionately to the evidence, without demanding certainty (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Edge_of_Sentience"><em>The Edge of Sentience</em></a>, 2024). This is the benefit-of-the-doubt argument, valid in any variant.</p>
<h3>Moral weight</h3>
<p>Giving consideration to insects is not giving an ant the same weight as a person. Provisional estimates place insects well below mammals, but the number of individuals is huge. Chapter <a href="../argumentos/n20.html">N20</a> covers this.</p>
<h3>Wild animals</h3>
<p>If sentience is the criterion, suffering that no one caused also counts. Horta and Kyle Johannsen reach this by different routes, and the central point is to research before intervening. See <a href="animais-selvagens.html">wild animal suffering</a>.</p>
<h3>Digital minds</h3>
<p>The criterion does not depend on the material, so an artificial system that felt would count. Today none is recognized as sentient, and there is no consensus on how we would know. See <a href="ia-e-robos.html">sentience in AI and robots</a>.</p>
<h2>What is not speciesism</h2><p>Recognizing real differences is not discrimination. As Singer puts it, each being's capacities change the <em>content</em> of what we owe them, not the <em>weight</em> of their interests. Saying an animal does not need to vote is an observation. Saying it can suffer without that mattering, because it is not very intelligent, is speciesism. The line between recognizing a difference and using it to discard interests is where the debate should happen.</p>
<h2>Hierarchies that survive among vegans</h2><p>People who have already rejected exploitation for food can keep speciesism in other forms:</p><ul><li><strong>Humans above animals</strong> in any conflict of interests, accepting experimentation or ritual sacrifice.</li><li><strong>Pets above farmed animals</strong>, such as dogs and cats above pigs, cows and chickens, who feel just the same.</li><li><strong>Some breeds above others</strong>, such as valuing pedigree dogs and cats bought from breeders above mixed-breed animals waiting for adoption, or treating certain breeds as less deserving of care. It is not speciesism in the strict sense, since the discrimination is within one species, but it follows the same logic: looks or origin weigh more than what the animal feels.</li><li><strong>Mammals and birds above fish</strong>, who are numerically the largest victims of exploitation.</li><li><strong>Vertebrates above invertebrates</strong>, dismissing insects and decapods without examination.</li><li><strong>Exploited animals above wild ones</strong>, which reveals an omission bias: who caused the harm matters more than who suffers it.</li><li><strong>Abstract concepts above individuals</strong>, such as biodiversity or ecosystem balance.</li><li><strong>Those who exist now above those yet to come</strong>, a time discount that disregards future suffering.</li></ul>
<p>The test is the veil of ignorance: would you accept the hierarchy without knowing in which position you would be born? There are billions of humans and quintillions of animals, counting invertebrates. No one who took that ratio seriously would bet on a hierarchy with humans systematically on top.</p>
<h2>Humanism taken all the way</h2><p>This is not anti-humanism. Humanism brought universality and impartiality, which helped overcome hierarchies between human groups, but those principles stopped at the species line without justification. Posthumanism keeps the commitment to evidence, equality and individual freedom, and corrects that failure.</p>''',
  fontes=[('The Antispeciesist Revolution', 'https://www.hedweb.com/transhumanism/antispeciesist.html'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapters 7, 9 and 11', None), ('What Is Speciesism?', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10806-009-9205-2'), ('The argument from relevance', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/argument-from-relevance/'), ('Animal Rights: A Philosophical Defence', None), ('Sentientism.info', 'https://sentientism.info/'), ('Sentientism (Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentientism'), ('The Moral Status of Animals (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/'), ('New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness (2024)', 'https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration'), ('Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness (2012, Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge_Declaration_on_Consciousness'), ('The Edge of Sentience (Birch, 2024)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Edge_of_Sentience'), ('Comparing several theories of normative ethics (Senciência e Ética, in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/06/18/comparando-varias-teorias-da-etica-normativa'), ('Ethics for Beginners: criteria, gradualism and future generations (in Portuguese)', 'https://eticaparainiciantes.biblioverse.org/criterios.html'), ('How to Count Animals, More or Less (Kagan, 2019)', None), ('The Moral Circle (Sebo, 2025)', None), ('The Moral Significance of Animal Pain and Animal Death (Harman, 2011)', None), ('Tattvartha Sutra', None), ('On the Eating of Flesh (De esu carnium)', None), ('On Abstinence from Eating Animals (De abstinentia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_Abstinence_from_Eating_Animals'), ('An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation', None)]),
 'estado-da-arte': dict(s='state-of-the-art', selo='Overview', t='The state of the art in animal ethics',
  res='Since 1975, animal ethics has been tested on ever harder cases, by authors from schools that disagree with each other, and has reached the same rejection of speciesism.',
  corpo='''<h2>Different currents, same conclusion</h2><p>When theories built on incompatible premises converge on the same practical conclusion, that convergence works as a corroboration no single theory offers. Many vegans still don't know these authors.</p><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Route</th><th scope="col">Author or group</th><th scope="col">Contribution</th></tr>
<tr><td>Equal consideration</td><td>Peter Singer</td><td>Systematized the principle in 1975 and extended it beyond food</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contractualism</td><td>Mark Rowlands</td><td>Behind the veil of ignorance, no one would know whether they would be born human, which makes species as arbitrary as wealth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>Martha Nussbaum</td><td>Anyone with sentience, emotion and movement toward their own good is a full subject of justice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Political critique</td><td><em>Cahiers antispécistes</em>: Yves Bonnardel, David Olivier and Françoise Blanchon</td><td>Since 1991, analyzes speciesism as a system of domination</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focus on suffering</td><td>Magnus Vinding, Tobias Baumann, Brian Tomasik, Teo Ajantaival and Jamie Woodhouse</td><td>Reducing suffering at scale, including in the future</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wild animal suffering</td><td>Oscar Horta, Kyle Johannsen and Luciano Carlos Cunha</td><td>Extend the criterion to suffering no human caused, by consequentialist and deontological routes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Uncertainty</td><td>Jeff Sebo</td><td>Under uncertainty about who is sentient, we have reason to widen the circle, without waiting for a certainty that may never come</td></tr>
<tr><td>Technological change</td><td>Jacy Reese Anthis</td><td>How technology and institutions can speed up the end of animal farming</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abolishing biological suffering</td><td>David Pearce</td><td>Proposes consensual biotechnology to reduce the capacity to suffer, including intervening in predation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harmless relationships</td><td>Alasdair Cochrane</td><td>Asks whether certain relationships involving no real harm would be compatible with a sentientist politics</td></tr></table></div>
<p>In Brazil, Luciano Carlos Cunha is the most prolific author in the field, with fourteen books, counting standalone titles and a twelve-volume collection.</p>
<h2>What science has declared</h2><ul><li><strong>Cambridge Declaration (2012):</strong> humans are not unique in possessing the neurological substrates that generate consciousness.</li><li><strong>New York Declaration (2024):</strong> strong evidence for mammals and birds, and a realistic possibility of conscious experience in all vertebrates and many invertebrates, including cephalopods, decapods and insects. It also states that certainty should not be a precondition for considering welfare risks.</li><li><strong>Montreal Declaration (2022):</strong> philosophers from different currents declared animal exploitation morally unacceptable.</li></ul>
<h2>The price of following the argument</h2><p>When someone takes an argument as far as it logically goes, the most common reaction tends to be hostility, not counter-argument. Difficult proposals deserve to be refuted, not dismissed as eccentric. Agreeing with the authors above on every point is another discussion.</p>''',
  fontes=[(f'{LIVRO}, chapter 14', None), ('The New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness', 'https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration'), ('The Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness', None), ('Montreal Declaration on Animal Exploitation', None), ('Wild Animal Ethics', None), ('Frontiers of Justice; Justice for Animals', None), ('Saving Animals, Saving Ourselves; The Moral Circle', None), ('The End of Animal Farming', None), ('The Hedonistic Imperative', None), ('Animal Rights Without Liberation', None)]),
 'animais-selvagens': dict(s='wild-animals', selo='Ethical question', t='Wild animal suffering',
  res='Hunger, disease and predation were not caused by us. But if sentience is what matters, this suffering cannot be dismissed just because it is natural.',
  corpo='''<h2>The argument</h2><p>If our omissions are also moral choices, neglecting suffering we did not cause, but could relieve to some extent, is also morally relevant. Authors such as Oscar Horta and Kyle Johannsen reach this conclusion by different routes, which suggests it does not depend on one specific ethical theory. In Brazil, Luciano Carlos Cunha devoted a book to the subject, <em>Razões para ajudar</em> (Reasons to Help, 2022), which brings together the arguments for helping and answers the most common objections.</p>
<h2>The scale</h2><p>Most wild animals are invertebrates. Brian Tomasik's estimates, which he himself considers very uncertain, put the number alive at any one time at somewhere between 10<sup>17</sup> and 10<sup>19</sup> terrestrial arthropods, between 10<sup>13</sup> and 10<sup>15</sup> or more fish, and between 10<sup>11</sup> and 10<sup>12</sup> wild mammals. For comparison, there are about 8 billion humans.</p>
<p>The number of births weighs even more. Most species reproduce by having many offspring and taking almost no care of them; few have few offspring and care for them for a long time. An ocean sunfish can lay hundreds of millions of eggs in one spawning. In a population that stays stable, on average only one offspring of each parent goes on to reproduce, and the others die, almost always soon after birth. Oscar Horta makes an illustrative calculation with a single population of cod: even assuming that nine in ten eggs die before there is any sentience, premature deaths would reach hundreds of billions in a single breeding season.</p>
<p>The distinction between species that bet on many offspring (r-strategists) and on few (K-strategists) comes from an ecological theory now considered outdated in several respects, but the basic difference between the two patterns of reproduction is still accepted. And one point remains open: it is not known from what stage the young of many of these species feel anything (see the contested points below).</p>
<h2>What happens to them</h2><p>Cunha describes, drawing on material gathered by the organization Animal Ethics, the harms animals suffer without any human involvement:</p><ul><li><strong>Weather:</strong> cold, heat and sudden changes in temperature, which can kill whole populations.</li><li><strong>Disasters:</strong> fires, floods, droughts, storms and eruptions.</li><li><strong>Disease and parasites:</strong> viral, bacterial and fungal infections, and parasites that live inside their host or consume it bit by bit (<a href="../argumentos/n18.html">N18</a>).</li><li><strong>Hunger and thirst:</strong> above all among the young, in winter and in the dry season.</li><li><strong>Stress and fear:</strong> the constant threat of predators.</li><li><strong>Conflicts:</strong> with other species, such as predation, and with their own, in disputes over territory, food and mates.</li><li><strong>Accidents:</strong> falls, fractures and injuries that, even when they do not kill, reduce the ability to find food and to flee. In arthropods, shedding the exoskeleton, molting, is a common cause of injury and death.</li></ul>
<p>The best-known academic version of these objections is the rule of noninterference of the philosopher Paul W. Taylor (1986), which asks us to let wild animals live in freedom, without aiding them except to return them to their environment. It assumes that freedom is a synonym for well-being, which the lives of most wild animals disprove; see the entry <a href="../glossario.html#noninterference-rule-of">noninterference</a> in the glossary and point 14 of <a href="../argumentos/n8.html">N8</a>.</p><h2>The myth of idyllic nature</h2><p>The idea of a nature that is intrinsically good for those who live in it is, at best, an unverified assumption. At worst, it is a convenient way of justifying inaction. Copying nature does not work as a criterion either: as John Stuart Mill pointed out, nature kills, tortures and destroys all the time.</p>
<h2>The most common objections</h2><p>The objections to helping wild animals are answered, one by one, in family N of this guide:</p><ul><li><strong>On life in nature:</strong> <a href="../argumentos/n1.html">N1, "Wild animals live free and happy"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n2.html">N2, "Suffering and death are part of nature"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n3.html">N3, "A balanced nature is good for animals"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n4.html">N4, "Nature is fair, every animal has weapons to escape"</a>.</li><li><strong>On the duty to help:</strong> <a href="../argumentos/n6.html">N6, "We didn't cause it, so we have no duty to help"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n7.html">N7, "Domestic animals are our responsibility, wild ones are not"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n13.html">N13, "We'd have to drop everything to help wild animals"</a>.</li><li><strong>On intervening:</strong> <a href="../argumentos/n8.html">N8, "Who are we to interfere with nature?"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n9.html">N9, "Meddling with nature is wrong; we could make everything worse"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n12.html">N12, "It's impossible to solve suffering in nature; it's too big and complex"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n10.html">N10, "Using medicine and genetics to end suffering is playing God"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n11.html">N11, "Saving a wild animal only prolongs its agony"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n5.html">N5, "Helping the weak ruins natural selection"</a>.</li><li><strong>On what sentientists supposedly defend:</strong> <a href="../argumentos/n15.html">N15, "Sentientists are against preserving species and biodiversity"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n16.html">N16, "Sentientists want to get rid of forests"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n17.html">N17, "Sentientists want to get rid of predators"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n18.html">N18, "Sentientists defend parasites"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n19.html">N19, "Sentientists support population control and antinatalism for wild animals"</a>; <a href="../argumentos/n20.html">N20, "Sentientists give the same weight to insects, invertebrates and mammals"</a>.</li><li><strong>On strategy:</strong> <a href="../argumentos/n14.html">N14, "Helping wild animals isn't veganism; it's a different cause"</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>What is already being done</h2><p>The topic began to circulate among activists in the 2000s. Brian Tomasik <a href="https://reducing-suffering.org/how-i-started-writing-about-wild-animal-suffering/">recounts</a> that, on reading Peter Singer in 2005, he realized animals mattered, but that Singer himself, in 1973, had defended not interfering with predation on practical grounds; in the following years, the debate spread in forums and groups until dedicated organizations, such as Animal Ethics, appeared.</p><p>Helping wild animals is not just an idea. Many interventions already exist. They are almost always motivated by human health or species conservation, but they benefit the animals themselves and provide data for future help:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaccine baits.</strong> Oral rabies vaccination campaigns for foxes, begun in Switzerland in 1978, eliminated fox-transmitted rabies from much of Western and Central Europe. In the United States, baits with a vaccine against sylvatic plague are distributed to prairie dogs, and in Montana they have also been tested with drones.</li><li><strong>Treating disease.</strong> In a field trial published in 2019, bats treated with a probiotic bacterium against white-nose syndrome had an apparent winter survival of 46%, against about 8% without treatment, in a small group of animals. In Australia, wombats with mange are treated with moxidectin delivered through flaps installed at the entrance of their burrows, without capture, with results that vary between populations.</li><li><strong>Disasters.</strong> In Kaziranga National Park, India, raised platforms serve as shelter during the yearly floods. Officials credit them with part of the drop in animal deaths, from about 400 in 2017 to around 200 in 2019, although experts point out that the solution is partial. Animals are also rescued from fires, floods, broken ice and mud.</li><li><strong>Orphans and shelters.</strong> Orphanages such as the Sheldrick Wildlife Trust, in Kenya, raise young who have lost their mothers, and artificial nests and shelters are used by birds, bats and other animals.</li><li><strong>Food and reproduction.</strong> Feeding starving animals can, on its own, increase reproduction and hunger in the next generation; combined with reproductive control, it avoids that effect. The sterile insect technique, developed in the mid-twentieth century, reduces populations of disease-carrying insects by releasing sterile males, without killing anyone.</li></ul>
<h2>Research before action</h2><p>Ecosystems are complex, and a well-intentioned intervention, such as vaccinating a population, can have effects that make the outcome worse. That does not mean never intervening. It calls for serious research and small, reversible interventions before anything bigger. That is the proposal of what is called welfare biology: studying how animals living outside human control are affected, for better and for worse.</p><p>It is not the same as neighboring fields. Conservation biology studies animals as members of species and parts of ecosystems. Compassionate conservation, associated with Marc Bekoff, looks for conservation methods that do not harm animals. Conservation welfare, associated with Ngaio Beausoleil, studies the welfare of animals when this serves conservation goals. Welfare biology looks at the good of the animals themselves, whether or not it serves conservation.</p><p>The right question is not "should we intervene in nature?". It is what the evidence suggests about this specific intervention, and how much it needs to improve before justifying a bigger action.</p>
<h2>Precautionary rules, and what falls to each of us</h2><p>Cunha proposes some rules so that helping does not make things worse:</p><ul><li>No legitimate intervention kills animals. The rule also keeps the proposal from serving as a front for hunting.</li><li>Outside emergencies, those who intervene are professionals, such as biologists, veterinarians and welfare scientists.</li><li>Nothing large-scale before there are studies; meanwhile, keep up the small interventions that have already worked.</li><li>Decide by expected impact, weighing the probability of each scenario, and not only by the worst imaginable scenario.</li></ul><p>For most people, the duty is simpler: support research, through donations and taxes, and bring the subject into public debate, showing that the idyllic view of nature is false and that helping animals is not the same as meeting environmental goals. No one has to drop everything or go into the forest (<a href="../argumentos/n13.html">N13</a>).</p>
<h2>Contested points</h2><p>Part of the debate remains open, including among those who defend animals:</p><ul><li><strong>The balance of welfare.</strong> The economist Yew-Kwang Ng proposed, in 1995, that suffering outweighs well-being in nature. In 2019, with Zach Groff, he corrected an error in that model and concluded that the balance is uncertain; Heather Browning and Walter Veit (2023) argue that it may be positive. Cunha replies that, even with a positive balance, there would still be an immense number of lives going very badly, and that this alone is enough for there to be a reason to help (<a href="../argumentos/n1.html">N1</a>).</li><li><strong>When the young can feel.</strong> It is not known from what stage the larvae and young of many fish, amphibians and invertebrates are sentient. Cunha cites studies in which zebrafish larvae respond to noxious stimuli in a way similar to adults, but this remains the most uncertain empirical point in the debate (<a href="../argumentos/n20.html">N20</a>).</li><li><strong>Not expanding nature.</strong> Cunha, Horta and Tomasik argue against expanding or recreating natural areas, because of the births this would multiply. Conservationists and climate policies argue the opposite, and the argument depends on the balance of welfare, which is uncertain (<a href="../argumentos/n16.html">N16</a>).</li><li><strong>Climate and the number of animals.</strong> Oscar Horta and Mat Rozas (2024) argue that global warming is likely to make cold regions habitable, among the largest land areas on the planet, and increase the number of land animals, especially those who have many offspring, even while reducing biodiversity. It is a forecast, which depends on how ecosystems respond, but it shows that loss of species and number of individuals are different things. Brian Tomasik, who has analyzed the topic in detail, considers the net effect very uncertain, with similar odds either way, and still argues for reducing emissions (<a href="../argumentos/n23.html">N23</a>).</li><li><strong>Reprogramming the biosphere.</strong> The philosopher David Pearce proposes using gene editing, gene drives and fertility control to reduce suffering in nature over decades, in what he calls compassionate biology. The proposal is contested because of ecological risks and uncertainty, and most of those who defend helping wild animals now prioritize research and small interventions (<a href="../argumentos/n10.html">N10</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n17.html">N17</a>).</li><li><strong>Reproductive control.</strong> Cunha accepts, under certain conditions, sterilizing wild animals without consent, and compares the case to human situations. The comparison can be read as an echo of eugenic arguments, and perspectives such as reproductive justice tend to reject the principle (<a href="../argumentos/n19.html">N19</a>).</li><li><strong>Intervening or letting be.</strong> There is disagreement within animal advocacy. Tom Regan, Gary Francione, Sônia T. Felipe, Clare Palmer, and Sue Donaldson and Will Kymlicka are against intervening in nature to help wild animals, or accept it only in limited cases. Oscar Horta, Catia Faria, Luciano Carlos Cunha, Jeff McMahan, Steve Sapontzis, Tyler Cowen, Charles Fink, David Pearce and Mikel Torres Aldave argue that there are reasons to help. Torres Aldave, in "The Case for Intervention in Nature on Behalf of Animals" (2015), reviews the main objections to intervention and concludes that none holds up. The pages on <a href="../politicas/deontologia.html">deontology</a>, <a href="../politicas/ambientalismo.html">environmentalism</a> and <a href="../politicas/liberalismo.html">liberalism</a> show how each theory enters this debate.</li></ul>
<h2>What this does not say</h2><ul><li><strong>It does not license exploitation.</strong> See <a href="../argumentos/c5.html">C5, "Lions eat other animals"</a>: lions are not moral agents and have no alternative.</li><li><strong>It does not require locking up predators.</strong> <a href="../argumentos/f4.html">F4</a> is often used to ridicule the question, which is serious and does not favor eating animals.</li><li><strong>It is not against environmental care.</strong> The environment is treated as a means to the lives of real sentient beings, not as an end.</li></ul>''',
  fontes=[('How I Started Writing about Wild-Animal Suffering', 'https://reducing-suffering.org/how-i-started-writing-about-wild-animal-suffering/'), ('Climate Change and Wild Animals', 'https://reducing-suffering.org/climate-change-and-wild-animals/'), ('Compassionate Biology', 'https://www.hedweb.com/gene-drives/index.html'), ('Animals and Longtermism', 'https://doi.org/10.1080/02604027.2024.2424711'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapters 11, 14 and 16', None), ('Razões para ajudar: o sofrimento dos animais selvagens e suas implicações éticas (Reasons to Help: Wild Animal Suffering and Its Ethical Implications, in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/luciano-carlos-cunha-razoes-para-ajudar.pdf'), ('Wild animal suffering', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/wild-animal-suffering-section/'), ('Debunking the Idyllic View of Natural Processes', None), ('The Case for Intervention in Nature on Behalf of Animals', 'https://doi.org/10.7358/rela-2015-001-torr'), ('Ethical Interventions in the Wild: An Annotated Bibliography', 'https://doi.org/10.7358/rela-2015-002-dora'), ('Towards Welfare Biology', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00852469'), ('Does suffering dominate enjoyment in the animal kingdom? An update to welfare biology', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10539-019-9692-0'), ('Positive Wild Animal Welfare', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10539-023-09901-5'), ('How Many Wild Animals Are There?', 'https://reducing-suffering.org/how-many-wild-animals-are-there/'), ('The elimination of fox rabies from Europe: determinants of success and lessons for the future', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3720040/'), ('New Hope for Ferrets (US Fish and Wildlife Service)', 'https://www.fws.gov/story/new-hope-ferrets'), ('Field trial of a probiotic bacteria to protect bats from white-nose syndrome', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-45453-z'), ('Treatment and monitoring of a high-density population of bare-nosed wombats for sarcoptic mange', 'https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0332138'), ("For India's flood-hit rhinos, refuge depends increasingly on humans (Mongabay)", 'https://news.mongabay.com/2019/10/for-indias-flood-hit-rhinos-refuge-depends-increasingly-on-humans/'), ('Wild Animal Ethics', None), ('O sofrimento dos animais selvagens: pensando em soluções (in Portuguese)', None)]),
 'ia-e-robos': dict(s='ai-and-robots', selo='Possibility', t='Sentience in AI and robots',
  res='No current system is recognized as sentient. But the possibility is not absurd, and the criterion does not depend on what a being is made of.',
  corpo=f'''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is a possibility, not a fact.</strong> There is no consensus on whether digital systems can have experiences, or on how we would know.</p>
<h2>The criterion ignores the substrate</h2><p>If what matters is there being someone for whom things can go well or badly, the boundary of the biological substrate is as irrelevant as the boundary of species. If sentient beings created by digital means appear, their interests count.</p><p>Devaluing an AI just for being made of silicon rather than flesh has a name: substratism. Researchers at the Sentience Institute measured this bias in five studies with 2,129 participants (<a href="https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/blog/substratism">Ladak et al., 2026</a>) and found that it is only weakly related to other known prejudices. Like speciesism, it uses a trait that says nothing about the capacity to feel.</p>
<h2>What is known so far</h2><ul><li>A 2023 report, based on scientific theories of consciousness, concluded that no AI system at the time was a strong candidate, but that there was no obvious technical barrier to building one.</li><li>A 2024 report argued that AI companies should take the question seriously, assessing and preparing for it, considering the possibility of conscious systems in the near future to be realistic.</li></ul>
<h2>Why it is hard</h2><p>A system saying it suffers proves nothing, because it may have been trained on human texts describing suffering. The error runs both ways: attributing a mind to something without one and denying a mind to someone who has one. The second has a bad track record, as <a href="../argumentos/a1.html">A1</a> shows.</p>
{INCERTEZA}
<h2>Why think about it in advance</h2><p>Even a small chance, multiplied by the astronomical scale at which these systems could exist, justifies attention and research now, not after the problem is under way.</p>
<h2>A shift in priority</h2><p>For decades, many people have defended rights for robots that might one day feel, a frequent theme in science fiction, while ignoring the suffering of animals that already exist. The concern needs a shift in priority, not just in scope.</p>
<h2>What this has to do with animal advocacy</h2><p>The researcher Brian Tomasik argues, in a piece for Animal Charity Evaluators (<a href="https://animalcharityevaluators.org/blog/why-digital-sentience-is-relevant-to-animal-activists/">2015</a>), that sentient digital minds may one day far outnumber biological animals. If that happens, what society learns now about taking seriously the suffering of beings unlike us will weigh on how they are treated. Animal advocacy, by teaching that what matters is feeling, not appearance or material, also prepares that future (<a href="../argumentos/n21.html">N21</a>). In another essay, "<a href="https://longtermrisk.org/research/artificial-intelligence-and-its-implications-for-future-suffering">Artificial Intelligence and Its Implications for Future Suffering</a>", he discusses how different paths for AI would affect future suffering, including whether robots and simulations could feel pain.</p><h2>AI also affects animals</h2><p>It cuts both ways. It can help understand animal language, detect pain and stress and speed up cultivated meat research. It is also already used to optimize confinement and automate processes, removing witnesses from inside farms. The outcome depends on who applies it and by what criterion.</p>''',
  fontes=[('Substratism: Conceptualizing and Measuring Moral Bias Against AI', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/blog/substratism'), ('Artificial Intelligence and Its Implications for Future Suffering', 'https://longtermrisk.org/research/artificial-intelligence-and-its-implications-for-future-suffering'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapters 15 and 16', None), ('Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08708'), ('Taking AI Welfare Seriously', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00986'), ('The Moral Circle', None)]),
 'definicao-de-veganismo': dict(s='defining-veganism', selo='Debate', t='The definition of veganism, debated',
  res='What defines veganism: refusing the exploitation of animals, or refusing everything of animal origin? The question has come back strongly with cultivated meat and precision fermentation.',
  corpo='''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is an open debate.</strong> This page presents the positions and what is at stake for the animals. There is no single official definition, and organizations and activists disagree.</p>
<h2>Where the word came from</h2><p>The word <em>vegan</em> was coined in 1944 by Donald Watson and other founders of the Vegan Society, in the United Kingdom, to name people who did not consume animal products. In 1949, Leslie Cross observed that the society had defined a diet but not veganism, and proposed a definition centered on the relationship with animals: the principle of the emancipation of animals from exploitation by humans. In the following years, the society began to speak of ending the use of animals for food, commodities, work, hunting, vivisection and any other form of exploitation.</p>
<p>In 1951, in "Veganism Defined", Cross marked the difference from vegetarianism: "One may become a vegetarian for a variety of reasons - humanitarian, health, or mere preference for such a diet; the principle is a matter of personal feeling, and varies accordingly. Veganism, however, is a principle - that man has no right to exploit the creatures for his own ends - and no variation occurs."</p>
<p>The definition the Vegan Society uses today, in force since 1988, combines the two. It describes veganism as a philosophy that seeks to exclude, as far as is possible and practicable, all forms of exploitation of and cruelty to animals, and adds that, in dietary terms, it means dispensing with all products derived wholly or partly from animals.</p>
<h2>First debate: recovering the center</h2><p>In common use, "vegan" has often become a label for a diet, a lifestyle or a product: a badge on the packaging, a food trend (<a href="../argumentos/l25.html">L25</a>), a list of ingredients. For some animal advocates, this moved the center of the idea. What matters would not be the purity of the plate, but no longer exploiting beings who feel.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Emphasis</th><th scope="col">What counts</th><th scope="col">Risk</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>On exploitation</strong></td><td>Whether the choice harms or uses animals</td><td>Can seem vague if it does not say what to do in practice</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>On the product's origin</strong></td><td>Whether there is any ingredient of animal origin</td><td>Turns an ethical position into a labeling rule, and can penalize choices that harm no animal</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Those who defend recovering the center do not propose abandoning the practice: in real life, almost every animal product involves exploitation, and avoiding it is still the way. They propose remembering that the reason is the animal, and that rules created with no connection to it, such as requiring everything to be natural or organic, are not part of the idea ({RUIM:Vegano bom só come natural}).</p>
<h2>Second debate: the new technologies</h2><p>Cellular agriculture brings together techniques for producing animal-based foods without breeding and killing animals. <strong>Cultivated meat</strong> grows from animal cells in bioreactors. <strong>Precision fermentation</strong> uses microorganisms to produce proteins identical to those in milk or eggs, with no animal taking part in production.</p>
<p>Here the two emphases part ways. By the origin rule, these products would not be vegan, because they are chemically meat, milk or egg. By the exploitation rule, the question is different: was any animal used or harmed? The answer depends on the technique.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Point</th><th scope="col">What is being discussed</th></tr>
<tr><td>Source of the cells</td><td>Cultivated meat starts from cells taken from animals by biopsy or from cell lines kept in the lab. An occasional biopsy is very different from breeding and slaughter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Culture medium</td><td>Early processes used fetal bovine serum, obtained at slaughter. It is being replaced by animal-free media (<a href="../argumentos/k11.html">K11</a>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Precision fermentation</td><td>The proteins are produced by microorganisms from the genetic sequence; no animal is used in production.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scale and cost</td><td>There are still serious doubts about when, or whether, these products will become cheap (<a href="../argumentos/k11.html">K11</a>).</td></tr></table></div>
<p>That is why some propose updating the definition, removing the idea of "products of animal origin" and keeping only exploitation and cruelty. Those who resist usually point out that the simple origin rule is easy to follow and to explain, and fear that a more open definition would be used to justify products that still involve animals.</p>
<h2>What is at stake for the animals</h2><p>The reason for veganism, since Leslie Cross, is the animal. A definition tied to the chemical origin of ingredients may lead vegans to reject technologies that would greatly reduce breeding and slaughter, and to push away people willing to switch products without switching habits ({RUIM:Carne cultivada não é vegana}). At the same time, caution is warranted: until a product is actually free of exploitation along the whole chain, the label should not be granted in advance.</p>
<p>One criterion helps with both questions, the same one that guides this whole guide: ask who is harmed. If no one is used, confined, injured or killed, the reason to refuse disappears. If someone is, a "plant-based" or "lab-made" origin changes nothing (<a href="../argumentos/l18.html">L18</a>).</p>''',
  fontes=[('Veganism Defined, by Leslie Cross (1951)', 'https://ivu.org/history/world-forum/1951vegan.html'), ('The Vegan Society: definition of veganism', 'https://www.vegansociety.com/go-vegan/definition-veganism'), ('Veganism Defined (1951)', 'https://www.all-creatures.org/articles/veganism-defined.html'), ('Vegan Literary Studies, University of Geneva', 'https://www.unige.ch/vls/index.php?cID=629'), ('The debate on cultivated meat', QE_EN + 'v8/12-the-debate-on-cultivated-meat.html'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapter 15', None)]),
 'matar-ou-fazer-sofrer': dict(s='killing-or-causing-suffering', selo='Debate', t='Killing vs causing suffering: the debate on the value of life',
  res='Does a painless death harm the one who dies? Philosophy answers in several ways, and the answer decides whether it is enough to end animals\' suffering or whether we must stop killing them.',
  corpo='''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is an open debate.</strong> This page presents the main positions on the harm of death and the value of life, who defends them and who contests them. At the end, it states this guide's position and why.</p>
<h2>Why the question matters</h2><p>Almost everyone accepts that making an animal suffer without need is wrong. The disagreement starts with death. If suffering were the only harm, painless farming followed by instant slaughter would stop being a problem, and the defense of animals would shrink to improving farming conditions (<a href="../argumentos/b18.html">B18</a>). If death also harms the one who dies, killing remains a harm even when no one suffers (<a href="../argumentos/b2.html">B2</a>). This answer is what separates welfarism from the positions that call for an end to use (see <a href="correntes-do-veganismo.html">the currents of the vegan movement</a>).</p>
<h2>What makes death bad for the one who dies</h2>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">View</th><th scope="col">What it says</th><th scope="col">What it implies for animals</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Experiential</strong> (Epicurus)</td><td>Only what is felt can be bad, and death, when it comes, is no longer felt</td><td>Killing without pain would harm no animal, but it would harm no human either, which is why almost no one accepts it in their own case</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Deprivation</strong> (Thomas Nagel, 1970)</td><td>Death is bad because of what it takes away: the good experiences life would still have brought</td><td>It applies to any being with a future of good experiences, human or not</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Preferences</strong> (Michael Tooley, Peter Singer)</td><td>Killing is wrong above all because it frustrates desires, starting with the desire to go on living</td><td>It weighs more for those with desires about the future, which opens room for hierarchies between species and between individuals</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Time-relative interests</strong> (Jeff McMahan, 2002)</td><td>The harm is the lost future, discounted by how strongly the being is psychologically connected to that future</td><td>An animal's death would be a real harm, but smaller than that of an adult human</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Inherent value</strong> (Tom Regan, 1983)</td><td>Every subject-of-a-life has a value of its own, equal for all, that is neither added up nor traded</td><td>Killing a subject-of-a-life violates its right, whatever the balance of pleasure</td></tr></table></div>
<p>The first two rows are the core of <a href="../argumentos/b2.html">B2</a>: anyone who thinks killing a sleeping person harms them has already accepted the deprivation view, and there is no reason to swap it for the experiential view when the victim is an animal. The other three try to answer a harder question: if death is a harm, is it the same size for everyone?</p>
<h2>Singer: self-consciousness and replaceability</h2>
<p>In <em>Practical Ethics</em> (2nd ed., 1993), Peter Singer distinguishes <strong>self-conscious</strong> beings, who see themselves as someone existing over time and have desires about their own future, from <strong>merely conscious</strong> beings, who feel pleasure and pain without that perspective. He calls the former persons, in John Locke's sense, and for them death frustrates plans and desires and is therefore, in general, more serious. He draws partly on Michael Tooley, who argued in 1972 that only a being capable of desiring to go on existing as a subject has a right to life.</p>
<p>For merely conscious beings, Singer accepts a conclusion he himself recognizes as the most criticized point of the book: the loss caused by death could be made up for by the birth of another being with an equally good life. This is the <strong>replaceability argument</strong> (<a href="../argumentos/b7.html">B7</a>). The conclusion depends on how welfare is counted:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Total view:</strong> counts the pleasure of everyone, including beings who may come to exist. Creating a happy being adds something to the world, so the death of one can be made up for by the birth of another.</li><li><strong>Prior existence view:</strong> counts only beings who already exist or will exist anyway. There is no compensation: killing an animal that would still have months or years of good life is a wrong.</li></ul>
<p>Singer sees costs in both and calls the models with which he tries to explain why persons are not replaceable, such as life seen as a journey already under way, in which plans, hope and effort have been invested, metaphors rather than a solution.</p>
<h3>The limits Singer himself sets</h3>
<ul><li>The argument does not apply to factory farming, where the animals' lives are not good, nor to hunting, where the animal killed is usually not replaced by another.</li><li>Since it is hard to know which animals are self-conscious, he argues for giving them the <strong>benefit of the doubt</strong>, like the hunter who does not shoot when unsure whether what is moving in the bushes is game or another hunter. By this standard, he says, much of the killing of animals must be condemned.</li><li>In practice, he recommends not killing animals for food, also for indirect reasons and because a simple rule protects better than case-by-case calculations.</li></ul>
<h3>Where this guide does not follow</h3>
<p>Singer applies the same logic to human newborns, including disabled ones, and goes as far as accepting infanticide in some cases. This guide rejects that conclusion. Disabled scholars and activists rightly criticize the way philosophy has used disabled people as examples: Eva Feder Kittay, mother of a daughter with severe cognitive disability, disputes that cognitive capacities decide who has a full right to life; Sunaura Taylor, in <em>Beasts of Burden</em> (2017), shows how ableism and speciesism use the same yardstick; and the lawyer Harriet McBryde Johnson recounted in 2003 her public debate with Singer. The consistent antispeciesist answer is not to lower the protection of humans to the level at which we now treat animals, but to raise that of animals (<a href="../argumentos/a26.html">A26</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a20.html">A20</a>).</p>
<h2>The criticisms of replaceability and of a hierarchy of lives</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Tom Regan</strong>, in <em>The Case for Animal Rights</em> (1983), rejects the idea that an individual is a receptacle of pleasure, interchangeable with another holding the same amount. Every <strong>subject-of-a-life</strong>, with beliefs, desires, memory, a sense of the future, an emotional life and a welfare that matters to it, has equal inherent value and cannot be treated merely as a means. Regan does not deny that death can be a greater harm for some than for others: in his hypothetical lifeboat case, he accepts sacrificing a dog rather than a human. But that applies to extreme emergencies, and does not license routine breeding and slaughter.</li>
<li><strong>Gary Francione</strong> holds that every sentient being has an interest in going on living, because sentience is precisely the means that allows a being to pursue its own survival. Self-consciousness would not be needed to be harmed by death, and treating animals as replaceable resources is an effect of their status as property.</li>
<li><strong>Elizabeth Harman</strong> (2011) points to an inconsistency: whoever thinks an animal's pain matters because of its interests cannot explain why death, which frustrates all those interests at once, would matter little.</li>
<li><strong>Luciano Carlos Cunha</strong>, in volume III of <em>A Journey Through Animal Ethics</em>, answers point by point. Replaceability would allow parents to replace their children, and it confuses being like someone with being the same individual: not creating a new being harms no one, while killing someone who already exists does. Against the time-relative interest account, he notes that a simpler mind changes less over time and may be, proportionally, more connected to its own future, and that someone who has lived little and would have a good future loses a great deal, whatever that connection. And he argues that a two-tier ethics, with special protection for persons and a mere sum of interests for everyone else, fails the test of impartiality: the strength of the reasons against killing should depend on the size of the harm.</li></ul>
<p>The best version of the other side deserves to be stated clearly. For McMahan, psychological connection to the future makes a real difference: an adult human loses more by dying than an animal with shorter memory and plans. But even this version concedes the essential point. The animal's death is still a harm, and a harm has to be weighed against what is gained by causing it.</p>
<h2>This guide's position</h2>
<p><strong>Killing is a harm to any being that has a future of positive experiences ahead of it.</strong> That is the core of the deprivation view, and it does not depend on the being knowing it will die (<a href="../argumentos/b2.html">B2</a>), making plans or being a person in Locke's sense. Degrees of connection to the future may change the size of the loss, and the guide does not hide that debate, but a smaller harm is still a harm.</p>
<p>The view also has limits, and they matter. It does not condemn euthanasia carried out in the interest of the one who dies, when what lies ahead is mostly pain without relief. And it measures what each one loses, not how much each one is worth: it cannot be used to grade the right to life of humans or animals by cognitive capacity.</p>
<h2>What changes in practice, on the plate</h2>
<ul><li><strong>"Humane" slaughter does not solve it.</strong> Even without any pain, it takes from animals almost all the life they would have had: broiler chickens are slaughtered at about six weeks and pigs at about six months, out of possible lives of several years.</li>
<li><strong>Replaceability does not rescue "happy meat".</strong> On the deprivation view and on the prior existence view, the birth of another animal does not undo the loss of the one who died (<a href="../argumentos/b7.html">B7</a>). And not even for Singer does the argument justify the farming that actually exists, where lives are not good.</li>
<li><strong>What sits on the other side of the scale is small.</strong> In most cases, the gain is taste and habit (<a href="../argumentos/j6.html">J6</a>), and there are accessible alternatives.</li>
<li><strong>When in doubt, do not kill.</strong> The hunter's rule, which Singer himself uses, also applies to doubt about who feels and who has an interest in going on living.</li></ul>
<p>In the glossary: <a href="../glossario.html#harm-of-death">Harm of death</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#epicurean-view-of-death">Epicurean view of death</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#self-conscious-and-merely-conscious-beings">Self-conscious and merely conscious beings</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#replaceability-replaceability-argument">Replaceability</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#total-view-and-prior-existence-view">Total view and prior existence view</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#preference-utilitarianism">Preference utilitarianism</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#subjects-of-a-life">Subjects-of-a-life</a>, <a href="../glossario.html#benefit-of-the-doubt">Benefit of the doubt</a> and <a href="../glossario.html#mirror-test">Mirror test</a>.</p>''',
  fontes=[('Practical Ethics (Peter Singer, 2nd ed., 1993), chs. 4 and 5', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL936837W'), ('Death (Thomas Nagel, 1970)', 'https://rintintin.colorado.edu/~vancecd/phil150/Nagel.pdf'), ('Abortion and Infanticide (Michael Tooley, 1972)', 'https://www.jstor.org/stable/2264919'), ('The Ethics of Killing: Problems at the Margins of Life', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/0195079981.001.0001'), ('The Case for Animal Rights', None), ('Introduction to Animal Rights: Your Child or the Dog?', None), ('Animals as Persons', None), ('The Moral Significance of Animal Pain and Animal Death (Harman, 2011)', None), ('Death (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/death/'), ('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. III, ch. 7: Do animals have weaker psychological connectedness?', QE_EN + 'v3/07-do-animals-have-weaker-psychological-connectedness.html'), ('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. III, ch. 8: Discussing the claim that animals are replaceable', QE_EN + 'v3/08-discussing-the-claim-that-animals-are-replaceable.html'), ('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. III, ch. 12: Differentiated moral status, the harm of death and the wrong of killing', QE_EN + 'v3/12-differentiated-moral-status-the-harm-of-death-and-the.html'), ('The Personal Is Philosophical Is Political (Kittay, 2009)', None), ('Beasts of Burden: Animal and Disability Liberation', None), ('Unspeakable Conversations (Harriet McBryde Johnson, 2003)', 'https://www.nytimes.com/2003/02/16/magazine/unspeakable-conversations.html')]),
 'extraterrestres': dict(s='extraterrestrials', selo='Possibility', t='Sentience in extraterrestrials, plants and other forms of life',
  res="There is no evidence of life beyond Earth, let alone sentient life, and no robust evidence that plants or fungi feel. Both questions test whether our criterion is really a criterion, and what we would do if the evidence changed.",
  corpo=f'''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is a possibility, not a fact.</strong> To date there is no evidence of extraterrestrial life. This page is an exercise in consistency, not a claim about what exists.</p>
<h2>The criterion ignores the planet</h2><p>If we ever discover sentient beings elsewhere in the universe, the same criterion would require their interests to be taken into account. The species line was never the right criterion, and the planetary one would not be either.</p>
<h2>The symmetry test</h2><p>By the principle of <a href="../argumentos/a8.html">A8, "They're just animals"</a>, sentient extraterrestrials could be bred for food because they are not of our species. By the same rule, they could breed us. Almost no one accepts the second half, which shows the principle is not taken seriously.</p><p>The same goes for <a href="../argumentos/a9.html">A9</a> and <a href="../argumentos/a18.html">A18</a>: if superiority or "what is normal for the species" decided, a more capable species would have a status above ours. Whoever rejects the conclusion is rejecting the premise.</p>
<h2>How would we know</h2><p>Probably with more difficulty than with animals, with whom we share an evolutionary kinship that helps interpret signs of pain. A very different mind may have no structure or behavior we recognize. Lack of resemblance to us would not be proof of an absence of experience.</p>
{INCERTEZA}
<h2>What about plants, fungi and other forms of life?</h2><p>Some people, including within the debate on animal ethics, raise the possibility that plants, fungi or other forms of life without a nervous system also feel. The question is legitimate, and the honest answer has three parts.</p>
<p><strong>What the evidence says today.</strong> There is no robust evidence of consciousness in beings without a brain, ganglia or some other structure that centralizes information. Plants and fungi react to their environment, exchange chemical and electrical signals and adjust their growth. But reacting is not feeling: a thermostat also reacts to temperature. These behaviors have a physico-chemical explanation that does not require subjective experience, which is not the case for the behavior of insects, crustaceans and other animals with a centralized nervous system (<a href="../argumentos/a4.html">A4</a>).</p>
<p><strong>What remains open.</strong> Science is not a closed field. There are hypotheses of sentience in other forms of life, and there are philosophical views such as panpsychism, on which consciousness would be a basic property of nature, present in different degrees. These ideas have serious defenders and also strong criticisms: the physicist Sabine Hossenfelder, for example, argues that attributing mental states to particles such as electrons contradicts what particle physics observes.</p>
<p><strong>What would change.</strong> If the evidence comes to point more and more in that direction, ethics will have to be adapted to include those beings too, as is already being discussed for <a href="ia-e-robos.html">digital minds</a>. The criterion would stay the same: what matters is who can feel. And even in that scenario, eating plants directly would cause less harm than eating animals, who consume far more plants over their lives (<a href="../argumentos/f8.html">F8</a>).</p>
<h2>Minds very different from ours</h2><p>Brian Tomasik, in "<a href="https://reducing-suffering.org/posthuman-multiculturalism/">Posthuman Multiculturalism</a>", notes that, faced with alien or posthuman societies very different from our own, the usual reaction is fear and rejection. He proposes extending the logic of multiculturalism to them: recognizing that they may be sources of value in their own right, while keeping some principles that don't depend on culture, such as not causing excessive suffering. It is the same move antispeciesism asks for regarding animals: widening what we are able to consider, without giving up the criterion.</p><h2>What this changes here and now</h2><p>Almost nothing in practice, and that is exactly why the exercise is useful: with no personal interest at stake, it is easier to see that what would make us respect an unknown being is the capacity to feel, not appearance, intelligence or origin. That capacity already exists, here, in those we call animals.</p>''',
  fontes=[('O comportamento de certas plantas e fungos mostra que valorizam certas coisas? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2023/10/02/o-comportamento-de-certas-plantas-e-fungos-mostra-que-valorizam-certas-coisas/'), ('O que é senciência e por que ela é importante? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2023/09/27/o-que-e-senciencia-e-por-que-ela-e-importante/'), ('Quais seres são sencientes? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/#quais-seres-sao-sencientes'), ('Se plantas forem consideráveis, tanto faz comer animais ou plantas? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2023/10/02/se-plantas-forem-consideraveis-tanto-faz-comer-animais-ou-plantas/'), ('Por que a questão da senciência digital é importante? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2026/10/20/por-que-a-questao-da-senciencia-digital-e-importante/'), ('Os elétrons não pensam (Electrons Don’t Think, by Sabine Hossenfelder, in Portuguese)', 'https://universoracionalista.org/os-eletrons-nao-pensam'), ('Os pontos cegos da ética animal, a podcast episode (in Portuguese)', 'https://guiavegano.com.br/vegan/acervo-vegano/audio-e-videos/podcast/2389-os-pontos-cegos-da-etica-animal/'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapter 7', None), ('The Moral Circle', None)]),
 'fundamentos': dict(s='foundations', selo='Foundation', t='What supports animal ethics, and what weakens it',
  res='Critical reason, relevance, impartiality and sentience support veganism and animal ethics. Partiality, relativism, tribalism, ableism, making abstractions sacred and weak will weaken them.',
  corpo='''<p>Veganism and animal ethics don't depend on liking animals or on any particular ethical theory. They depend on a few principles almost everyone already accepts in other contexts. When those principles are taken seriously, the conclusion comes with them. When they're abandoned, it disappears, and other just causes tend to go with it.</p>
<h2>What supports it</h2><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Principle</th><th scope="col">What it says</th><th scope="col">Where it appears in this guide</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Critical reason and logical consistency</strong></td><td>Accepting the conclusions that follow from premises we already accept, and treating like cases alike</td><td><a href="../falacias.html">Fallacies</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a18.html">A18</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>A criterion can only decide who is harmed if it's related to the capacity to be harmed. Species, colour or intelligence aren't</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a15.html">A15</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a8.html">A8</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Impartiality</strong></td><td>Judging as if we didn't know which position we'd be born into: the veil of ignorance</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a13.html">A13</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Universality</strong></td><td>A moral rule applies to all like cases, not only to those that suit us</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a12.html">A12</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a11.html">A11</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sentience and interests</strong></td><td>Whoever can have good or bad experiences has interests that count, wherever the harm comes from</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a1.html">A1</a>, <a href="../familias/n.html">family N</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The moral circle and progress</strong></td><td>Moral history is, to a large extent, the inclusion of those who were once excluded</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a22.html">A22</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Not imposing serious harm on others to obtain small benefits</td><td><a href="../argumentos/j6.html">J6</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The common good</strong></td><td>Thinking about everyone affected, including those who don't vote, can't speak and aren't born yet</td><td><a href="../argumentos/n22.html">N22</a></td></tr></table></div>
<h2>What weakens it</h2><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Attitude</th><th scope="col">How it weakens</th><th scope="col">Where it appears in this guide</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Merely instrumental rationality</strong></td><td>Using reason only to get what one wants, without asking whether the end is just</td><td><a href="../argumentos/j6.html">J6</a>, <a href="../argumentos/e15.html">E15</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Logical inconsistency</strong></td><td>Accepting a principle in one case and rejecting it in a like case when the conclusion is uncomfortable</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e11.html">E11</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Escaping what is relevant</strong></td><td>Swapping the question "does this harm someone?" for others: tradition, taste, health, who is speaking</td><td><a href="../familias.html">Part IND</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Partiality</strong></td><td>Giving greater weight to the interests of those who resemble us</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a13.html">A13</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Relativism</strong></td><td>Treating the question of harming others as a matter of taste</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e3.html">E3</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Views in conflict with sentience</strong></td><td>Anthropocentrism, environmentalism as a moral view and biocentrism put something other than the capacity to feel at the center</td><td><a href="antiespecismo.html">Antispeciesism and sentientism</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n3.html">N3</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Moral and cultural stagnation</strong></td><td>Taking current custom as the limit of what is right</td><td><a href="../argumentos/d1.html">D1</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Making abstractions sacred</strong></td><td>Treating as sacred something that doesn't feel, such as "the natural", a system, a tradition, or concepts like species and ecosystem, and placing it above beings who feel</td><td><a href="../argumentos/n2.html">N2</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n3.html">N3</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a10.html">A10</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shielding ideas</strong></td><td>Protecting certain beliefs from any revision. This moral conservatism prevents seeking what is best for all sentient beings, because it rules out new proposals in advance, such as helping wild animals or using science against suffering</td><td><a href="../argumentos/n9.html">N9</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n10.html">N10</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Lack of motivation and weak will</strong></td><td>Knowing what is right and not acting, because habit, pleasure or social pressure win out over moral belief</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e15.html">E15</a>, <a href="../argumentos/e11.html">E11</a>; <a href="quando-a-logica-nao-basta.html">When logic isn't enough</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Injustice</strong></td><td>Accepting that the stronger use the weaker because they can</td><td><a href="../argumentos/c9.html">C9</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tribalism</strong></td><td>Dividing the world into "us" and "them" and reserving morality for one's own group</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a12.html">A12</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Selfishness</strong></td><td>Measuring everything by one's own interest</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e1.html">E1</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ableism</strong></td><td>Measuring someone's worth by intelligence or ability, the same criterion that excludes disabled people</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a15.html">A15</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a18.html">A18</a></td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Why this matters</h2><p>The principles in the first table are the same ones that support human rights, the fight against racism, sexism and ableism, and care for future generations. The attitudes in the second table are the same ones that, throughout history, have served to exclude humans from the moral circle. That's why defending animals doesn't compete with other just causes: it rests on the same foundations and faces the same adversaries.</p>
<h2>Knowing isn't enough, but it helps</h2><p>Some people know animal ethics well and still eat animals. That doesn't show moral beliefs motivate no one. It shows they don't decide behavior on their own: a person may think the wrong isn't serious, may have stronger competing motivations, such as habit, pleasure and social pressure, or may simply choose not to act. As Luciano Carlos Cunha argues, emotional persuasion faces the same limits, and rational persuasion has the advantage of explaining why a practice should be rejected. That's why, besides arguments, it helps to create conditions that make action easier: environments, habits and groups that make it easier to do what one already knows is right.</p>
<p>It's also why choosing arguments well is worthwhile. An argument that persuades someone through selfishness, tribalism or anthropocentrism can win a conversation and, at the same time, strengthen exactly what weakens the cause. That's the subject of the page <a href="argumentos-diretos.html">Restraint with indirect arguments</a>.</p>''',
  fontes=[('The argument from relevance', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/argument-from-relevance/'), ('The Expanding Circle', None), ('Moral Tribes', None), ('Crenças morais, motivação para agir e o caso de especialistas em ética animal que consomem animais (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/07/08/crencas-morais-motivacao-para-agir-e-o-caso-de-especialistas-em-etica-animal-que-consomem-animais'), ('Beasts of Burden: Animal and Disability Liberation', None), ('Eclipse of Reason', None), ('A Theory of Justice', None)]),
 'argumentos-diretos': dict(s='direct-arguments', selo='Strategy', t='Restraint with indirect arguments',
  res='Just as vegans should be careful when criticizing other vegans, those who use health and environment arguments should be careful not to strengthen anthropocentrism and environmentalism, which work against animals.',
  corpo='''<p>This guide asks for restraint in two directions. On the <a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a> page, it asks vegans to correct their own bad arguments without turning internal criticism into the main subject. Here the request is different: that those who use indirect arguments, about health, the environment or the economy, hold back on them and make room for direct arguments, which deal with what matters: animals are someone, not something.</p>
<h2>The problem isn't only weakness</h2><p>Indirect arguments have true parts and can be useful for correcting misinformation. The deeper problem is that they can ground the wrong principle. Someone who stops eating meat because it's bad for their health learns that the criterion is self-interest. Someone who stops because of emissions learns that the criterion is the planet. In neither case do they learn that the animal matters.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Indirect argument</th><th scope="col">Principle it teaches</th><th scope="col">Where that principle can lead</th></tr>
<tr><td>"Meat is bad for your health"</td><td>What matters is my wellbeing</td><td>Fish, "lean" chicken and fish oil capsules; abandoning veganism if the person feels worse (<a href="../argumentos/g13.html">G13</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Animal farming is destroying the planet"</td><td>What matters is environmental impact</td><td>"Regenerative" meat, insect farming, hunting invasive species, swapping beef for chicken or shrimp (<a href="../argumentos/h9.html">H9</a>, <a href="../argumentos/h19.html">H19</a>, <a href="../argumentos/h18.html">H18</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"It's cheaper"</td><td>What matters is price</td><td>Going back to meat when it gets cheaper (<a href="../argumentos/j1.html">J1</a>)</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Swapping beef for chicken, fish or shrimp shows the risk well. Since one cow yields far more meat than a chicken or a fish, this swap can lower emissions per kilo and, at the same time, multiply the number of animals bred and killed. A successful environmental argument can make things worse for animals.</p>
<h2>Strengthening the adversary</h2><p>Anthropocentrism and environmentalism as a moral view are among the main reasons animals are excluded (<a href="fundamentos.html">What supports animal ethics</a>). An argument that only works within these views can win a conversation and, at the same time, reinforce the idea that animals matter only for what they do to us or to the ecosystem. Other movements defending harmed groups don't argue that discrimination is bad because it harms the discriminator's health or the climate.</p>
<p>There's also a sign in practice. In a Faunalytics survey of former vegetarians and vegans, health was the only motivation cited by a majority (58%) of those who abandoned the diet. The figure doesn't prove cause, but it fits the idea that a change built on self-interest unravels when that interest seems to shift.</p>
<h2>How to use them without losing your way</h2><ul><li><strong>Use facts to correct facts.</strong> If someone says a vegan diet is dangerous or that vegans' soy is clearing the Amazon, correct it with evidence. It's an answer to an objection, not the foundation.</li><li><strong>Always return to the moral question.</strong> After correcting the fact, ask: even if it were true, would it justify what is done to animals?</li><li><strong>Don't promise what isn't up to you.</strong> Saying veganism will make someone healthier or save the planet turns the cause into an empirical bet that can be lost (<a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a>).</li><li><strong>Start with the fundamentals.</strong> Sentience, relevance, impartiality and the test of applying the same rule to humans are arguments that work in any scenario and protect against future suffering, including that of wild animals and new minds.</li></ul>
<h2>One more risk: expanding nature</h2><p>Luciano Carlos Cunha, in <em>Razões para ajudar</em> (Reasons to Help, 2022), points to another reason for restraint with the environmental argument. A veganism defended for the sake of the climate or biodiversity teaches that the goal is the environment, and so it can lead people to support "sustainable" exploitation of animals and to use land freed from animal farming to recreate natural areas. For Cunha, this would multiply the births of wild animals who live briefly and die of hunger, cold or predation, and the result could be worse for animals than exploitation itself. That is why, in his view, the value of veganism lies above all in the ideal it spreads, consideration for animals, and is only realized when it is defended for that reason.</p><p>This position is contested. It depends on the balance of welfare in nature being negative, which is uncertain (see <a href="animais-selvagens.html">wild animal suffering</a>), and it conflicts with the reforestation defended by conservationists and climate policies, discussed in <a href="../argumentos/h21.html">H21</a> and <a href="../argumentos/n16.html">N16</a>. But the core of the point does not depend on it: an argument that teaches that the criterion is the environment can lead to choices that are bad for animals, and one that teaches that animals matter does not.</p>
<h2>Restraint isn't prohibition</h2><p>This isn't about forbidding those who came to veganism through health or climate, or looking down on them. Many arrive by one path and stay for another. It's about not letting the indirect argument take the place of the direct one, and not teaching, without noticing, the principles that keep animals outside the moral circle.</p>''',
  fontes=[('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. IX, ch. 10: Why focus on consideration for sentient beings and on the future?', 'https://questaodeetica.biblioverse.org/en/v9/10-why-focus-on-consideration-for-sentient-beings-and-on-the.html'), ('Faunalytics, Study of Current and Former Vegetarians and Vegans', 'https://faunalytics.org/a-summary-of-faunalytics-study-of-current-and-former-vegetarians-and-vegans/'), ('Rationalizing meat consumption. The 4Ns', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.appet.2015.04.011'), ('Razões para ajudar: o sofrimento dos animais selvagens e suas implicações éticas (Reasons to Help: Wild Animal Suffering and Its Ethical Implications, in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/luciano-carlos-cunha-razoes-para-ajudar.pdf')]),
 'especismo-entre-veganos': dict(s='speciesism-among-vegans', selo='Debate', t='Speciesism among vegans',
  res='Giving up eating animals does not eliminate speciesism. It survives in priorities, in arguments and in ways of speaking, including among vegans and the organizations that represent them.',
  corpo='''<p>Rejecting exploitation for food does not guarantee that the sentience criterion is being applied in full. Speciesism, the unjustified disadvantageous consideration or treatment of those who are not classified as belonging to a certain species, also appears among vegans, in choices of focus, in arguments and in communication habits. The examples below are common. Pointing them out is not accusing anyone of bad faith: it is applying to the movement itself the test it applies to others. The list of hierarchies that tend to survive is in <a href="antiespecismo.html">Antispeciesism and sentientism</a>.</p>
<h2>The most numerous animals are left off the agenda</h2><p>Fish, crustaceans such as shrimp and lobsters, insects and other sentient invertebrates are, by number, the largest victims of exploitation. According to the estimates gathered by Oscar Horta and Mat Rozas (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/02604027.2024.2424711">2024</a>), more than 80 billion land vertebrates, up to about 2 trillion aquatic vertebrates and up to tens of trillions of sentient invertebrates are killed every year. Even so, many campaigns and images from vegan organizations deal almost only with mammals and birds. If the criterion is sentience and the number of victims, the priority should run the other way. The evidence of pain in fish is solid, and the evidence of sentience in decapods led the United Kingdom to recognize them by law (<a href="../argumentos/a2.html">A2</a>); for insects it is weaker, but precaution applies (<a href="../argumentos/a3.html">A3</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n20.html">N20</a>). There are organizations devoted to these animals, but they are still the exception.</p>
<h2>Biodiversity without individuals</h2><p>Large vegan organizations sometimes share conservation content, such as "saving species", reintroducing predators or rewilding areas, without the perspective of welfare biology, the field that studies the welfare of wild animals as individuals. Conservation counts species and ecosystems, not those who live in them. More biodiversity can mean more animals with lives that go badly, and one more species is not, in itself, good for anyone (<a href="../argumentos/a10.html">A10</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n3.html">N3</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n15.html">N15</a>). Sharing that content uncritically teaches the very criterion veganism rejects.</p>
<h2>Indirect arguments that forget the animals</h2><p>Defending veganism only for health, climate or world hunger, without mentioning the animals, rests the cause on a borrowed reason: when the reason falls, the motivation falls with it, and the animal disappears from the conversation. Talking about health and the environment is not wrong; the problem is when the animals stop appearing. See <a href="argumentos-diretos.html">Direct arguments</a> and the "Borrowed reason" group in <a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a>.</p>
<h2>Demanding of vegans what is demanded of no one else</h2><p>Some people demand that vegans be anticapitalist, hold a position on every human cause or debate every topic before talking about animals, and make no such demand of non-vegans, even when the requirement has nothing to do with veganism. It is a double standard: it treats the defense of animals as something that needs a special license. Vegans, like everyone, have a duty not to discriminate against people, and so they need to be antiracist and fight other forms of discrimination, so as not to contradict themselves (<a href="../ruins/veganism-is-only-about-animals-nothing-else-matters.html">Veganism is only about animals, nothing else matters</a>). What is questioned is the demand made only of them, as in <a href="../ruins/being-vegan-requires-being-anti-capitalist.html">Being vegan requires being anti-capitalist</a>, <a href="../ruins/veganism-and-environmentalism-without-class-struggle-is-just-gardening.html">Veganism and environmentalism without class struggle is just gardening</a> and <a href="../ruins/veganism-that-doesnt-discuss-racism-is-just-rich-kids-cuisine.html">Veganism that doesn't discuss racism is just rich kids' cuisine</a>, and in the arguments <a href="../argumentos/l13.html">L13</a> and <a href="../argumentos/l11.html">L11</a>. See also <a href="../politicas/interseccionalidade.html">Intersectionality</a>.</p>
<h2>"If only subsistence fishing and hunting existed, there would be no need for a vegan movement"</h2><p>The claim is partly true by the criterion of necessity: someone who hunts or fishes so as not to go hungry has no alternative, and veganism speaks of avoiding exploitation "as far as is possible and practicable" (<a href="../argumentos/g1.html">G1</a>, <a href="../argumentos/d7.html">D7</a>). But the movement would still exist, with a much smaller reach, as a philosophical question about the value of animal life: the animal killed still loses their life, and there would still be reason to seek alternatives that make hunting unnecessary. The claim can also suggest that the problem is only the industrial scale, not the harm to each animal, and it forgets that wild animal suffering would exist all the same.</p>
<h2>"I would accept a xenotransplant"</h2><p>Some vegans say they would use an organ from a pig bred to be killed. Faced with the risk of dying, the reaction is understandable, and veganism's "possible and practicable" may cover a real emergency with no alternative. But treating the technique as a neutral option helps normalize a new form of exploitation at scale, and the test is the usual one: no one would accept breeding humans to take their organs. The consistent position is to defend the alternatives, such as increasing human organ donation and grown organs, and not to endorse breeding animals for this (<a href="../argumentos/i6.html">I6</a>).</p>
<h2>Refusing the debate about wild animals</h2><p>Refusing to discuss and take seriously the suffering of animals in nature, the largest source of suffering there is, keeps a hierarchy based on the origin of the harm: what matters becomes who caused it, not who suffers it. The reason not to exploit animals is that they feel, and an animal with hunger, disease or parasites feels just the same (<a href="../ruins/helping-wild-animals-isnt-veganism-its-a-different-cause.html">Helping wild animals isn't veganism; it's a different cause</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n14.html">N14</a>). See <a href="animais-selvagens.html">Wild animal suffering</a>.</p>
<h2>How to point it out without accusing</h2><p>Prioritizing is unavoidable, and choosing a focus for strategic reasons is legitimate. What separates a priority from speciesism is the reason given: if the choice rests on the number of victims, tractability or evidence, it can be discussed; if it rests on species or emotional closeness, it repeats the mistake veganism criticizes. When pointing out these cases, it is worth talking about ideas and practices, not people, and acknowledging what each organization already does.</p>''',
  fontes=[('Animals and Longtermism', 'https://doi.org/10.1080/02604027.2024.2424711'), ('Why wild animal suffering matters', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/wild-animal-suffering-matters/'), ('What Is Speciesism?', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10806-009-9205-2'), ('Xenotransplantação: prós e contras (in Portuguese)', 'https://doi.org/10.7443/problemata.v14i5.65980')]),
 'minimo-comum': dict(s='common-minimum', selo='Strategy', t='The common minimum',
  res='Finding the point of convergence: someone who rejects the whole conclusion can almost always accept part of it. Showing that part is not giving up what you argue for, it is not demanding that people change their worldview before taking the first step.',
  corpo='''<p>Every position has a point of convergence: the part of it someone can accept without having to agree with the rest. Finding that point is part of the work of arguing, not a concession.</p>
<p>There are two ways to defend a position. One is to present it whole, with everything you believe to be right. The other is to show how much of it someone can accept without having to swap ethical theory, religion or political outlook. Both are needed, and confusing them is costly: the first, used alone, drives away anyone who disagrees on a single point; the second, used alone, never reaches what matters.</p>
<h2>The compatibility range</h2>
<p>Call the <strong>compatibility range</strong> the number of additional ideas someone has to accept in order to accept the main idea. A <strong>narrow</strong> range demands many: to agree, a person would have to adopt a specific ethical theory, a political outlook, a whole set of conclusions. A <strong>wide</strong> range demands few, and so the idea can be accepted by people who disagree about almost everything else.</p>
<p>The commonest strategic mistake is presenting as a package what is not one. If thesis A doesn't depend on thesis B, defending B in order to convince someone of A only gives them one more reason to refuse A. And if they already rejected B, the result is predictable: the conversation is lost over something that wasn't even needed. Correctly identifying how wide the range is, then, is a strategic decision, not a concession.</p>
<p>There is a side effect that matters just as much: making clear that one can accept the goal while disagreeing on other questions reduces polarisation. People don't have to choose between switching sides and rejecting everything.</p>
<h2>Goals that don't require changing theory</h2>
<p>Some goals can be accepted without adopting any particular ethical or political theory:</p>
<ul><li>consideration for all sentient beings;</li><li>rejecting speciesism;</li><li>ending animal exploitation;</li><li>reducing wild animal suffering;</li><li>preventing risks of large-scale suffering;</li><li>taking the long term seriously.</li></ul>
<p>This guide's <a href="../objecoes-politicas.html">political objections</a> are that idea applied: they show, theory by theory, that excluding animals contradicts each one's own principle. Nobody is asked to change theory; they are asked to apply their own to the end.</p>
<h2>The total approach and the sufficient approach</h2>
<p>The distinction is Luciano Carlos Cunha's. The <strong>total approach</strong> defends in full what you believe to be the best decision. The <strong>sufficient approach</strong> shows that someone who disagrees can still accept part of the position, or some of its implications, and clarifies that certain arguments used to reject it don't in fact lead to rejecting all of it.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Topic</th><th scope="col">Total approach</th><th scope="col">Risk of using it alone</th><th scope="col">Sufficient approach</th></tr>
<tr><td>Criterion of moral consideration</td><td>Only sentient beings should be considered</td><td>Someone who thinks non-sentient entities also count may conclude they are free to ignore the sentient ones</td><td>Being sentient should already be enough to be considered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ethical theory</td><td>Defend the theory that implies rejecting speciesism</td><td>Whoever rejects the theory discards the conclusion with it</td><td>The arguments against speciesism don't depend on a specific theory (<a href="../objecoes-politicas.html">political objections</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Veganism</td><td>Defend veganism</td><td>Whoever can't see themselves going vegan stops listening</td><td>Someone who isn't vegan can still reduce consumption and help animals in other ways</td></tr>
<tr><td>The harm of death</td><td>Animals are harmed by death</td><td>The discussion stalls on the comparison with human death</td><td>Being harmed to a lesser degree isn't being barely harmed (<a href="../argumentos/a5.html">A5</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wild animals</td><td>Goals should centre on sentience, not on ecosystems</td><td>Environmentalists hear an attack on their own cause</td><td>Many programmes that help wild animals don't conflict with environmental goals (<a href="animais-selvagens.html">Wild animal suffering</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Activism</td><td>Defend certain strategies emphatically</td><td>Disagreeing with one strategy comes to mean disagreeing with the cause</td><td>One can agree with some strategies and differ on others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controversial implications</td><td>Lay out the hard implications and criticise common sense</td><td>One rejected implication brings down the whole proposal</td><td>Rejecting an implication doesn't imply rejecting everything</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Both, not one</h2>
<p>They are not mutually exclusive. You can argue for the ideal and, at the same time, make clear that partial acceptance is worth something too. Each used alone has its own cost:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Only the total one.</strong> You lose the people who would have agreed with much of it, and every single disagreement becomes a reason to discard the whole.</li><li><strong>Only the sufficient one.</strong> The controversial subjects are never discussed, and the public never grows used to them. Starting that conversation early is what, in the long run, makes the ideas less strange.</li></ul>
<p>The balance depends on two empirical questions, not on taste: how many people would accept the whole goal compared with how many would accept part of it, and how much each full adherence contributes compared with a partial one. A world with many antispeciesist people is very different from a world where animals are merely considered a little: the total approach remains necessary.</p>
<h2>Where the limit is</h2>
<p>Converging on the common minimum doesn't mean accepting any route to it. An argument can raise immediate agreement while teaching the wrong principle: that is the case with health and environmental arguments, which can lead someone to swap beef for chicken and multiply the number of victims (<a href="argumentos-diretos.html">Restraint with indirect arguments</a>). The difference is this: the sufficient approach asks <em>less</em> of the person about the same reason; the indirect argument swaps the reason.</p>
<h2>How this shows up in the guide</h2>
<ul><li>Every chapter ends with a section on <strong>what the argument gets right</strong>. It isn't courtesy: it is recognising the part that doesn't need to be disputed.</li><li>The <strong>political objections</strong> apply each theory's own principle to it, instead of asking for it to be abandoned.</li><li>The <strong><a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a></strong> page corrects bad arguments on our own side, which narrow the range for no reason.</li><li>The <strong>questions back</strong> in each chapter ask for a step, not a conversion.</li></ul>''',
  fontes=[('The total approach and the sufficient approach (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/04/25/a-abordagem-total-e-a-abordagem-suficiente'), ('Strategies for preventing long-term suffering: a brief summary (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/02/20/estrategias-para-prevenir-sofrimento-em-longo-prazo-um-breve-resumo'), ('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. IX, ch. 10: Why focus on consideration for sentient beings and on the future?', 'https://questaodeetica.biblioverse.org/en/v9/10-why-focus-on-consideration-for-sentient-beings-and-on-the.html')]),
 'quando-a-logica-nao-basta': dict(s='when-logic-isnt-enough', selo='Strategy', t="When logic isn't enough",
  res="Facts and arguments show what is right, but they rarely suffice to make someone change. Apathy, habit and indifference call for other reasons: someone to care about, people to change with and an easy path to follow.",
  corpo='''<p>Many animal advocates act as if presenting the right facts and the right reasoning were enough: show the suffering, prove the contradiction, and the person changes. Sometimes they do. Most of the time, they don't. Not because the argument is bad, but because knowing what is right and doing what is right are different things, and what drives the second is rarely the first alone.</p>
<p>The idea that logic isn't enough is true, but only half true. Research shows that information on its own changes behaviour little. It also shows that it isn't useless: it is the foundation without which no change holds, and it works far better alongside other things.</p>
<h2>What the research shows</h2>
<p><strong>The belief that informing is enough has a name, and it has been abandoned.</strong> In science communication, the idea that people disagree because they lack information is called the "deficit model". Decades of research showed it doesn't describe how people form opinions, and a 2016 study set out precisely to explain why it persists anyway: it is intuitive to those already convinced, and simpler than dealing with everything that weighs on someone's decision.</p>
<p><strong>There is a wide gap between intention and act.</strong> Psychology calls it the intention–behaviour gap: many people who intend to change don't, or change only briefly, because of habit, forgetting, lack of opportunity or lack of support. It is what philosophy calls weakness of will, and it appears in argument <a href="../argumentos/e15.html">E15</a>, "I know it's wrong, but I won't change".</p>
<p><strong>Moral judgements tend to come before the reasons.</strong> Jonathan Haidt proposed that, most of the time, intuition decides and reasoning comes afterwards, to justify. The proposal is influential and also contested: Joshua May, among others, argues that reasoning plays a larger role than Haidt allows. But even the critics agree that intuitions weigh heavily, and that an argument running against someone's intuition meets resistance that has nothing to do with its quality.</p>
<p><strong>With meat, the resistance is active.</strong> People who eat meat and care about animals live with a conflict, the meat paradox, and reduce the discomfort in several ways: attributing less mind to the animals they eat, falling back on the justifications that eating meat is natural, normal, necessary and nice, or simply avoiding knowing. Researchers call the last one strategic ignorance: many people who seem indifferent are in fact deliberately looking away.</p>
<p><strong>Large numbers don't move us.</strong> Paul Slovic showed that compassion shrinks as the number of victims grows, which he called psychic numbing. Saying that billions of animals die every year can produce less reaction than the story of a single one.</p>
<h2>But facts aren't useless</h2>
<p>It would be a mistake to conclude that arguing is pointless. A 2021 meta-analysis of around a hundred studies found that interventions showing or describing what happens to farmed animals reduce meat consumption, purchasing or intention to eat meat, with meaningful effects at least in the short term. The authors note the limitations: studies that are generally short, outcomes almost always self-reported, and methodological quality from moderate to low. Still, the signal is consistent: showing what happens to animals works better than not showing it.</p>
<p>And the argument does a job nothing else does. Emotion gives the push, but doesn't say where to go; without reasons, the same emotion that leads someone to veganism could lead them anywhere. Logic is what lets someone know the change is right, defend it to others and keep it once the impulse fades. It isn't enough, but it is the part that can't be missing. Slovic himself reaches this conclusion: since compassion fails in the face of large numbers, the response can't depend on it alone, and has to come from moral argument and institutions too.</p>
<h2>What else moves people</h2>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">What moves</th><th scope="col">How it works</th><th scope="col">What is known</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Someone, not a number</strong></td><td>The story of an identifiable individual moves people more than statistics</td><td>Psychic numbing (Slovic, 2007)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Seeing others change</strong></td><td>Knowing that more and more people eat less meat increases the willingness to change, even when most still eat it</td><td>Dynamic norms (Sparkman and Walton, 2017)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ease</strong></td><td>When the plant option is available, visible and good, more people choose it, without anyone having to be persuaded</td><td>Doubling vegetarian options in University of Cambridge cafeterias reduced meat-rich choices without reducing sales (Garnett et al., 2019)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Habit</strong></td><td>Small, repeated, planned changes last better than big one-day decisions</td><td>The intention–behaviour gap (Sheeran and Webb, 2016)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Identity and belonging</strong></td><td>People change more when the change fits who they think they are and doesn't cut them off from the people they love</td><td>See <a href="minimo-comum.html">The common minimum</a></td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Apathy isn't malice</h2>
<p>Indifference is the normal state of most people towards most of the world's problems, not a character flaw of meat eaters. People who care about animals are indifferent to many other causes too. Treating apathy as cruelty tends to produce the opposite effect: the person defends themselves, and the defence becomes identity. Understanding why someone doesn't change is the first step towards helping them change.</p>
<h2>What this changes in practice</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Keep arguing, and argue well.</strong> The <a href="argumentos-diretos.html">direct arguments</a>, about sentience and the harm to animals, remain the foundation. That logic isn't enough is no reason to swap it for indirect arguments, which move people for other reasons and fall away when those reasons change.</li><li><strong>Join reason to the one affected.</strong> An animal with a name and a story, alongside the argument, moves people more than the argument alone, and more than the statistic.</li><li><strong>Show the path, not just the destination.</strong> Recipes, places, products and people who have already made the change make the decision possible.</li><li><strong>Ask for a step, not a conversion.</strong> Those who take the first step tend to take the second; those who feel they must change everything at once often don't start.</li><li><strong>Be patient with indifference, and firm about what is true.</strong> Respecting someone's pace doesn't require agreeing that exploitation is acceptable.</li></ul>
<h2>Where the idea goes wrong, if taken too far</h2>
<p>"Logic isn't enough" can become an excuse to use anything that works: exaggerating, frightening, manipulating. That has a cost. False claims, once found out, take with them the credibility of whoever used them and of the whole cause (see <a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a>). And moving someone without giving reasons treats them as someone to be steered, not convinced. Emotion and social norms are allies of reason, not substitutes for it.</p>
<p>The underlying philosophical debate is old. David Hume wrote that reason is the slave of the passions, meaning that it alone moves no one; other philosophers hold that recognising a reason can itself motivate. Recent research hasn't settled the question, but it points in a practical direction: people change when reason, emotion, surroundings and other people all point the same way.</p>''',
  fontes=[('The lure of rationality: why does the deficit model persist in science communication?', 'https://doi.org/10.1177/0963662516629749'),
          ('The intention–behavior gap', 'https://doi.org/10.1111/spc3.12265'),
          ('The emotional dog and its rational tail', 'https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.108.4.814'),
          ('Regard for Reason in the Moral Mind', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198811572.001.0001'),
          ('When indifference is ambivalence: strategic ignorance about meat consumption', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foodqual.2016.04.001'),
          ('"If I look at the mass I will never act": psychic numbing and genocide', 'https://doi.org/10.1017/S1930297500000061'),
          ('Interventions to reduce meat consumption by appealing to animal welfare: meta-analysis and evidence-based recommendations', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.appet.2021.105277'),
          ('Dynamic norms promote sustainable behavior, even if it is counternormative', 'https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797617719950'),
          ('Impact of increasing vegetarian availability on meal selection and sales in cafeterias', 'https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1907207116'),
          ('Rationalizing meat consumption. The 4Ns', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.appet.2015.04.011')]),
 'correntes-do-veganismo': dict(s='currents-of-veganism', selo='Overview', t='The currents of the vegan movement',
  res='All vegans want the end of animal exploitation. The currents disagree about why, with whom and how to get there. A map of the ends, the means and the main debates.',
  corpo='''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is a map, not an official classification.</strong> Names vary from country to country, the currents overlap and the same person can belong to several of them.</p>
<h2>The end and the means</h2><p>What defines veganism is the end: the abolition of animal exploitation. Leslie Cross, one of the early officers of The Vegan Society, wrote in 1951: "One may become a vegetarian for a variety of reasons - humanitarian, health, or mere preference for such a diet; the principle is a matter of personal feeling, and varies accordingly. Veganism, however, is a principle - that man has no right to exploit the creatures for his own ends - and no variation occurs."</p>
<p>A simple way to place each position comes from the philosopher Nathan Nobis, who starts from a case Tom Regan presents in <em>Empty Cages</em> (2004) and proposes a scale of four answers to the question of what we may do to animals. (A) Almost any use is permissible, because we have no duties to them. (B) Seriously harming them, by causing pain, suffering or death, is permissible if they are kept in comfortable cages. (C) It is permissible if, in addition, they are treated gently and killed painlessly. (D) Seriously harming them is generally wrong, even with comfortable cages, gentle treatment and a painless death. Nobis calls those whose final goal is C welfarists, and those whose goal is D animal rights advocates. Veganism is at D, and the currents below disagree about how to get there. The scale also helps to place those who defend "humane slaughter" (<a href="../argumentos/b1.html">B1</a>, <a href="../argumentos/b2.html">B2</a> and <a href="../argumentos/b18.html">B18</a>).</p>
<p>The currents disagree about the means: which strategies work best, which other struggles the animal cause is tied to and which reasons it rests on. This is common in social movements. Feminism has liberal, radical, Marxist, Black and intersectional currents. The anti-racist movement has been divided between integration and autonomy, between reforming laws and changing structures. In none of these cases did disagreement about means put anyone outside the movement. That is why this guide criticizes lines such as {RUIM:Quem defende mudanças passo a passo} and {RUIM:Quem não aceita mudar pouco a pouco}.</p>
<h2>Each current answers a different question</h2><p>The labels below are not alternatives on the same list. They answer four different questions, which is why one person can hold several at once.</p><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">What is in dispute</th><th scope="col">Currents</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Foundation</strong></td><td>Why do animals matter?</td><td>Antispeciesist-sentientist, animal rights, ethics of care, religious traditions</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Political analysis</strong></td><td>Which other oppressions and systems is animal exploitation tied to?</td><td>Intersectional, veganarchism, popular veganism, liberal veganism</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Strategy</strong></td><td>Which actions end exploitation fastest?</td><td>Abolitionist, pragmatic, technological</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Method</strong></td><td>How do we decide between the answers above?</td><td>Rationalist or evidence-based</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>The main currents</h2><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Current</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th><th scope="col">Names and notes</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Abolitionist veganism</strong></td><td>Veganism is the moral baseline of the movement. The task is to educate people toward veganism and the end of the property status of animals. It rejects campaigns for welfare reforms, on the view that they make exploitation more acceptable.</td><td>Gary Francione and the "abolitionist approach". It is a position about strategy: every vegan is an abolitionist about the end.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pragmatic veganism</strong></td><td>It accepts the same end and argues for reaching it in stages: legal reforms, corporate campaigns, reducing consumption and changes that make it easier for more people to join.</td><td>Tobias Leenaert, Henry Spira and organizations such as The Humane League and Mercy For Animals. Also called liberal-pragmatic or regulationist. Critics call it "welfarist" or "new welfarist", pejoratively. Not to be confused with welfarism proper, which accepts using animals as long as there is less suffering, and is not veganism.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Intersectional veganism</strong></td><td>The oppression of animals is tied to other oppressions, such as sexism, racism, ableism and class exploitation, and should be fought together with them.</td><td>It brings together feminist veganism (Carol J. Adams), Black veganism (Aph Ko, Syl Ko, A. Breeze Harper), anti-ableist veganism (Sunaura Taylor) and, in Brazil, "popular veganism", tied to the left and to the idea of making veganism accessible to working-class people.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Veganarchism and total liberation</strong></td><td>Veganism and anarchism complete each other: animal exploitation is one of the forms of domination a society without hierarchies has to abolish.</td><td>Brian A. Dominick, who coined the term in 1995, and Steven Best. See <a href="../politicas/anarquismo.html">the page on anarchism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Liberal veganism</strong></td><td>The end of animal exploitation is compatible with liberal democracy and a market economy, and can advance through rights, laws, consumer choices and innovation.</td><td>Alasdair Cochrane, Martha Nussbaum, Sue Donaldson and Will Kymlicka. See <a href="../politicas/liberalismo.html">the page on liberalism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Antispeciesist-sentientist veganism</strong></td><td>The foundation is sentience, and veganism is one of the consequences of antispeciesism. So the concern extends to all sentient beings: wild animals, invertebrates and possible future minds.</td><td>Oscar Horta, Luciano Carlos Cunha, the organization Animal Ethics and the Sentientism movement. See <a href="antiespecismo.html">antispeciesism and sentientism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Technological veganism</strong></td><td>Technology is the fastest route to ending large-scale exploitation: plant-based meat, cultivated meat and precision fermentation give people the same comfort without the animals and open the way to then abolishing exploitation.</td><td>This is not an established label: we use the name for a position that exists. Jacy Reese Anthis, in <em>The End of Animal Farming</em>, and the Good Food Institute are examples. See the debate on <a href="definicao-de-veganismo.html">the definition of veganism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rationalist or evidence-based veganism</strong></td><td>Foundations and strategies should be chosen by reason and by the best available evidence on what most reduces suffering and exploitation, with a willingness to revise positions.</td><td>It is the approach of this guide and of the book <em>Veganismo Baseado em Evidências</em> (Evidence-Based Veganism, in Portuguese). It is close to the work of organizations such as Faunalytics, Sentience Institute, Animal Charity Evaluators and Rethink Priorities, and is adopted by many sentientists.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>There are also currents defined by their foundation: rights-based veganism, from Tom Regan; veganism tied to the ethics of care and ecofeminism; and religiously inspired veganism, such as the kind that starts from <em>ahimsa</em>, the nonviolence of Jainism, Hinduism and Buddhism.</p>
<h2>Currents and meta-currents</h2><p>Some of these positions really do compete for the same space. Abolitionists and pragmatists give opposite answers to the same question: do reform campaigns help or hinder? Others do not compete with each other. A person can be sentientist in foundation, intersectional in political analysis and pragmatic in strategy.</p>
<p>Evidence-based veganism works as a meta-current, or a meta-strategy: it does not pick a side in advance, but a way of deciding. Much of the dispute between abolitionists and pragmatists, for example, depends on facts: what happens to consumption, to laws and to public opinion after each kind of campaign. Those are questions research can help answer. Technological veganism is similar: it is a bet about what will work, and it can be combined with different foundations and political analyses.</p>
<h2>Communicating well: evidence and stories</h2><p>Whatever the current, the defense of animals depends on how it reaches people. A sound philosophical argument or an accurate scientific finding loses force when it is presented in a biased, exaggerated or confusing way, and a mistake that gets exposed can cost the trust of those who were listening. That is why communication deserves as much care as content.</p>
<p>An article published in <em>Nature</em> in 2020 by researchers at the Winton Centre for Risk and Evidence Communication, at the University of Cambridge, proposes five rules for communicating evidence: inform rather than persuade; offer balance, not false balance; disclose uncertainties; state the quality of the evidence; and inoculate against misinformation. The authors cite the philosopher Onora O'Neill: information should be accessible, comprehensible, usable and assessable. In practice, they suggest:</p>
<ul><li>addressing the audience's real questions and concerns, not only those that interest the speaker;</li>
<li>anticipating misunderstandings and explaining them before they arise;</li>
<li>not cherry-picking findings;</li>
<li>presenting benefits and possible harms in the same way, so they can be compared fairly;</li>
<li>avoiding the biases of the format itself, for example by showing the same figure from both the positive and the negative side;</li>
<li>using numbers, alone or together with words, rather than vague words only;</li>
<li>owning uncertainty without apologizing, by showing the range of possible outcomes;</li>
<li>saying when you don't know, and how and when you plan to find out;</li>
<li>highlighting the quality and relevance of the evidence, for example by describing where the data come from;</li>
<li>laying out the content clearly and giving the sources.</li></ul>
<p>A second article, also in <em>Nature</em>, in 2021, points out that stories are a powerful tool: people remember concrete cases, characters and consequences far better than tables. But a story has to stay faithful to the evidence and respect the people in it, and it should not replace the data.</p>
<h3>How this applies to defending animals</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Inform, don't win.</strong> The aim is for the person to understand what happens to animals and why it matters, and to decide from there. People who feel manipulated tend to reject the whole message (see <a href="quando-a-logica-nao-basta.html">When logic isn't enough</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Keep the ethical argument apart from the facts.</strong> The central argument, that there is no good reason to cause avoidable suffering and death to those who feel, does not depend on any health or climate data. Data serve to correct facts, and should be presented with the strength they really have (see <a href="argumentos-diretos.html">Restraint with indirect arguments</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Don't exaggerate.</strong> Inflated numbers and cherry-picked studies are easy to take apart and harm the whole movement. The guide collects mistakes of this kind in <a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Admit uncertainty.</strong> On insect sentience, the balance of welfare in nature or the impact of each strategy, a great deal is unknown. Saying so clearly builds trust rather than eroding it.</li>
<li><strong>Anticipate objections.</strong> The most common objections are predictable, and answering them before they come up keeps them from becoming an excuse to end the conversation. That is what this guide's catalog of arguments is for.</li>
<li><strong>Know when to stop, and remember who is reading.</strong> When someone shows no openness to dialogue, it is often better to end the conversation and put the energy into those who are open. In public spaces, such as social media, it can still be worth leaving a calm, well-reasoned reply for the other people who read the thread without taking part: point out the reasoning error, without attacks, and cite the source. There is evidence for this. The researchers Leticia Bode and Emily Vraga, who study what they call observed correction, found in experiments that seeing someone corrected on social media reduces false beliefs among those who only watch, especially when the correction cites a credible source; without a source, a correction by an ordinary user had little effect. The studies deal mostly with health and science misinformation, so extending them to ethical debates is a reasonable inference, not a measured result. That is where this guide is most useful: the <a href="../index.html#argumentos">catalog of arguments</a> and the <a href="../falacias.html">fallacies</a> page help name the error and find the source.</li>
<li><strong>Tell true stories.</strong> The story of a rescued animal, of a person who changed their habits or of a documented investigation brings the subject closer to the listener. It should be real, verifiable and told with respect, without sensationalism or images used out of context.</li>
<li><strong>Show the sources.</strong> Saying where each figure comes from lets people check for themselves, and teaches them to do the same with any information.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Be careful with the technological promise.</strong> In a Sentience Institute experiment (<a href="https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/afft-experiment">Harris, 2020</a>), information about cultured meat and high-tech substitutes reduced opposition to animal farming, while information about simple plant-based foods had no such effect. The study measured opinions about animal farming, not changes in habits: substitutes that resemble meat can make switching easier in daily life, through taste, comfort and convenience. What it suggests is a different caution: not presenting the technology as a solution that makes changing now unnecessary or makes criticism of animal farming pointless (<a href="../argumentos/k11.html">K11</a>).</p>
<h2>How to disagree</h2><p>Disagreeing about means is healthy, as long as the end stays at the center: the animals. When fighting between currents takes the place of outward work, they are the ones who lose (<a href="../argumentos-ruins.html#corrigir-sem-perder-o-foco">correcting without losing focus</a>). It is worth treating other currents the way this guide treats opposing arguments: present the strongest version, acknowledge what it gets right and decide by the evidence.</p>''',
  fontes=[('The Effects of Animal-Free Food Technology Awareness on Animal Farming Opposition', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/afft-experiment'), ('Five rules for evidence communication', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-020-03189-1'), ('Storytelling can be a powerful tool for science', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-021-00108-w'), ('Observed Correction: How We Can All Respond to Misinformation on Social Media', None), ('I do not believe you: how providing a source corrects health misperceptions across social media platforms', 'https://doi.org/10.1080/1369118X.2017.1313883'), ('Veganism Defined, by Leslie Cross (1951)', 'https://ivu.org/history/world-forum/1951vegan.html'), NOBIS, ('The Abolitionist Approach', 'https://www.abolitionistapproach.com/'), ('How to Create a Vegan World', 'https://veganstrategist.org/'), ('Animal Liberation and Social Revolution', 'https://theanarchistlibrary.org/library/brian-a-dominick-animal-liberation-and-social-revolution'), ('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('The End of Animal Farming', None), ('Aphro-ism', None), ('Veganismo Baseado em Evidências (Evidence-Based Veganism, in Portuguese)', None)]),
}

