# Short answer to the objection and the question to ask back, for the page and the answer image (keyed by the PT slug).
POL_CURTAS = {
 'liberalismo': ('Liberalism already protects those who are not autonomous, such as babies and people with profound cognitive disabilities: rationality was never the condition for protection. And the harm principle works against the exclusion. If animals can be harmed, and the evidence says they can, breeding and killing them is exactly the kind of action that affects others and that liberalism exists to limit.',
   'If a baby is not a rational individual, does liberalism stop protecting them from harm?'),
 'libertarianismo': ('Self-ownership rests on being a subject, someone with a life that can go well or badly, and sentient animals are subjects in that sense too. The non-aggression principle forbids initiating violence against those who have threatened no one, and breeding, mutilating and killing an animal is initiating violence. Saying the animal is property is the very conclusion in dispute, not a starting point.',
   'What aggression did the pig commit, for the violence against it not to count as aggression?'),
 'marxismo': ("The Marxist critique targets the turning of beings and lives into commodities, and billions of animals are bred, used and sold as commodities, on a scale no other exploitation reaches. Whether this counts as labor is a real debate, but it does not change the essentials. Ending the exploitation of animals does not compete with the workers' struggle, and industrial animal farming also exploits the humans who work in it.",
   'If exploitation is wrong because it turns lives into commodities, why does the life of an animal sold in pieces not count?'),
 'anarquismo': ('The relationship with farmed animals is one of the most complete hierarchies there is: one side decides when the other is born, where it lives, with whom it breeds and when it dies. Being unable to resist makes domination more serious, not less. And the anarchist tradition itself has made this link, from the geographer Élisée Reclus to the veganarchists.',
   'Does domination become legitimate just because the victim cannot organize against it?'),
 'utilitarismo': ("Bentham, one of the founders of utilitarianism, already said that the question is not whether animals can reason, but whether they can suffer. Mill's line compares kinds of pleasure, but it does not erase anyone's pain: even if intellectual pleasures were worth more, an animal's suffering would still count. And scale changes the sum: tens of billions of land animals and trillions of fish a year.",
   'If a higher pleasure is worth more, does the pleasure of a taste make up for a whole life of suffering?'),
 'contratualismo': ('The veil of ignorance exists to discount what is luck, such as race, sex and talent, and species is luck too. Not knowing whether you would be born a human or a chicken, nobody would approve rules that allow chickens to be bred and killed for a meal. And justice already protects babies and people with profound cognitive disabilities, who cannot make agreements either: being protected by a rule never required being able to follow it.',
   'Behind the veil, not knowing whether you would be born a human or a chicken, what rule would you choose for the farms?'),
 'deontologia': ('If only reason gave value, torturing an animal in secret, with no effect on anyone, would not be wrong, a conclusion almost nobody accepts. Many humans whom the theory protects as ends also lack moral autonomy. And interpreters of the Kantian tradition itself, such as Christine Korsgaard, argue that sentient animals are ends in themselves, because they have a good of their own.',
   'Would torturing a dog in secret, with nobody knowing, be wrong?'),
 'comunitarismo': ('Domesticated animals have lived inside our communities for thousands of years: they take part in our practices all the time, only as tools. Traditions also change, and many changed precisely to include those who were once outside. A community that defines its members by what suits it repeats the exclusion that the struggles for inclusion fought against.',
   'If the dog living in your home is not a member of the community, who decides who is?'),
 'igualitarismo': ('If priority goes to those who are worse off, animals in intensive farming and many wild animals are among the worst-off beings there are. Egalitarianism fights precisely the disadvantages nobody chose, and nobody chooses the species they are born into. Excluding animals is deciding in advance who can be worse off, which contradicts the principle itself.',
   "If priority goes to the worst off, why can't the worst off be an animal?"),
 'cuidado': ('Buying the chicken is a relationship: it is the choice that orders its life and death. Domesticated animals depend on us, because we made them that way, and care also guides what we do to those affected by our choices, not only to those we know by name. The feminist care tradition itself has already applied the concept to animals.',
   'Does anyone depend more on our choices than the animal we ourselves breed to eat?'),
 'virtudes': ("Compassion is directed at those who suffer: if the animal's suffering did not matter in itself, there would be nothing for it to respond to. A just and compassionate person who pays for the avoidable suffering of others acts against their own virtues. And when there is an alternative, causing someone's death for a meal is hard to reconcile with compassion.",
   'If compassion for the animal only serves to make us better, who exactly are we feeling compassion for?'),
 'feminismo': ("Milk and egg production is based on controlling the reproduction of females: insemination with no possibility of refusal, offspring taken away, slaughter when production falls. Autonomy over one's own body is at the heart of feminism, and it is exactly what these females lack. This does not equate women and cows: it points to a shared structure of control over bodies that feel.",
   "If controlling someone's body and reproduction is oppression, why does it stop being so when the body is a cow's?"),
 'antidiscriminacao': ("The comparison is not between victims but between structures of reasoning: in every case, a trait irrelevant to harm is used to discount interests. Each struggle has its own history and its own victims, and none needs to be diluted. But the principle behind them, not discounting someone's interests for a trait that does not change how much they can be harmed, does not stop at the species line.",
   'What trait of species changes how much an animal can be harmed?'),
 'esquerda-multiculturalismo': ('The criticism of selectivity is fair: campaigns have targeted minority practices and ignored the far more numerous practices of the majority. But that is an argument against selectivity, not against animals. Almost all animal deaths happen in exploitation carried out by majorities and large companies, such as industrial fishing and farming. And defending animals does not require denying anything to any human.',
   "If the problem is targeting only minority practices, wouldn't the answer be to start with the majority's industrial farming?"),
 'pacifismo': ('Breeding and slaughter are organized physical violence against beings who feel, not accidents. And there is no conflict at all: the violence is done only to obtain a product that has alternatives. Traditions of non-violence, such as ahimsa in Jainism and Hinduism, have always included animals. If violence is refused even in conflicts, it should be refused where there is no conflict at all.',
   'If violence is unacceptable even in a conflict, why would it be acceptable where there is no conflict at all?'),
 'altruismo-eficaz': ('The criteria of effective altruism, scale, neglectedness and tractability, favor the animal cause: the number of victims is huge and the resources devoted to it are small. Excluding animals in advance is deciding the result before doing the math, the opposite of what the approach proposes. Uncertainty about the weight of each animal exists, but it cuts both ways, and the scale is large enough to survive heavy discounts.',
   'If the rule is to do the math before choosing, why are animals left out of the math before it even starts?'),
 'ambientalismo': ('Ecosystems, species and biodiversity feel nothing: those who can be harmed are the individuals who live in them. The most common environmentalism is already centered on individuals, only human ones: it protects the climate and forests because people depend on them. If what makes people matter is that they feel, other beings who feel count too. And industrial farming and fishing are among the main causes of deforestation and habitat loss.',
   'If only the ecosystem matters, and not the individual, would you accept sacrificing humans to save a forest?'),
 'pragmatismo': ('If what counts is practical consequences, the first question is: consequences for whom? The suffering and death of billions of animals are real, measurable consequences, and leaving them out of the account is a choice made before looking at the results. What is feasible also changes because someone acts: conventional battery cages were banned in the European Union, and the sale of cosmetics tested on animals, in dozens of countries.',
   'Practical consequences for whom: only for those who vote?'),
}


