POL_CURTAS['interseccionalidade'] = ('Intersectionality shows that forms of discrimination intersect. If what unites them is giving less weight to someone because of a trait that doesn\'t change how much they can suffer, species is one more such trait, and animals fall within the principle. And it works both ways: vegans also need to fight discrimination between people, without that being demanded only of those who defend animals.',
   'If intersectionality opposes every hierarchy based on traits that don\'t change the capacity to suffer, why would it stop at species?')


# Added on 2026-11-20, from Luciano Carlos Cunha, Razões para ajudar (2022): what each theory says about helping wild animals.
# Mirrors SELVAGENS in politicas.py (same keys, same order of paragraphs). The section is appended to "mais" and the book and
# extra sources to "fontes", because the English entries replace those two fields wholesale.
RAZOES_EN = ('Razões para ajudar: o sofrimento dos animais selvagens e suas implicações éticas (Reasons to Help: Wild Animal Suffering and Its Ethical Implications, in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/luciano-carlos-cunha-razoes-para-ajudar.pdf')
_GROFF = ('Does suffering dominate enjoyment in the animal kingdom? An update to welfare biology', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10539-019-9692-0')
_BROWNING = ('Positive Wild Animal Welfare', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10539-023-09901-5')
POL_SELVAGENS = {
 'ambientalismo': ('Helping wild animals: where the conflict shows', [
    'The case of wild animals is where the difference between environmentalism and animal advocacy becomes clearest. The philosopher Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (Reasons to Help, 2022), separates three families of environmentalism by what they value directly: holism, which values wholes such as species and ecosystems (ecocentrism is the version centered on ecosystems); naturocentrism, which values what has a natural, non-human origin; and biocentrism, which values the good of each living organism. He notes that biocentrism, taken seriously, would call for more help for wild animals, not less: if the good of each organism counts, population dynamics, in which almost all die soon after birth, is a problem for it too (N15).',
    'Even for those who give species and ecosystems a value of their own, the conclusion "do not help" does not follow. First, an environmental value would only block all help if it worked as an absolute trump, not merely as a greater weight; and many kinds of help, such as vaccinating against rabies or pulling an animal out of the mud, affect no environmental goal at all. Second, if environmental value adds to the good of sentient beings, reducing suffering also increases the total value. Third, environmentalists themselves already limit their goals by the basic interests of humans: no one proposes leaving malaria untreated in a community that lives in the forest in order to preserve the natural course of things. Without speciesism, the same limit applies to other animals (N3, N9).',
    'Cunha goes further and holds that pluralist environmental views, which claim to give some value to sentient individuals, end up anthropocentric in practice, because they approve of culling animals for environmental goals but never humans. This is his thesis, and pluralists reject it. He also points out that ecology describes what happens in nature and does not say what we should do: those who draw a norm from it are the ones confusing the fields.',
    'The strongest point of conflict is the expansion of natural areas. Cunha, like Oscar Horta and Brian Tomasik, argues against expanding or recreating natural areas, for example through reforestation or rewilding, because this would multiply the births of animals that have thousands of offspring, almost all doomed to die young. He does not propose destroying existing habitats, and he advises against opening the conversation with this point when talking to someone who does not yet know the problem. The thesis is contested. Conservationists and climate policies defend reforestation, including as a way to remove carbon from the atmosphere; Cunha replies that there are other means against climate change, such as energy efficiency and renewable energy. Organizations devoted to wild animals, such as Wild Animal Initiative, currently focus on research into their welfare. And the thesis depends on a balance of welfare in nature that is uncertain: Zach Groff and Yew-Kwang Ng (2019) concluded that it could go either way, and Heather Browning and Walter Veit (2023) argue that it may be positive (N1, N16).',
    'J. Baird Callicott, who developed the land ethic from Aldo Leopold, is on the other side of this debate. In "Animal Liberation: A Triangular Affair" (1980), he set the holistic land ethic against the defense of animals as individuals. In "Animal Liberation and Environmental Ethics: Back Together Again" (1988), he brought the two closer by treating domesticated animals as members of a mixed community with humans, but he went on denying wild animals the right to life and treating them as parts of environmental goals. That is why he does not appear in the list below of those who defend animals from within this theory (A10).']),
 'utilitarismo': ('Wild animals in the utilitarian calculation', [
    'If what matters is suffering, and not who caused it, utilitarianism cannot ignore the suffering of wild animals, which comes above all from hunger, disease, parasitism and death soon after birth. The equivalence thesis, defended by James Rachels and, with qualifications, by Singer, says that there is no intrinsic difference between causing an avoidable harm and letting it happen, all else being equal (N6). Singer grants differences that usually come with the distinction, such as motivation and the certainty of the outcome, and so does not condemn the omission as if it were the act itself; but he holds that these differences are not enough to make it acceptable. And diminishing marginal utility means that the same resource does more good when it reaches those who are worse off, which strengthens the reason to help animals in extreme situations.',
    'Singer himself was skeptical: in Animal Liberation, he wrote that attempts to change ecosystems on a large scale tend to do more harm than good. According to Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), he changed his position in a 2016 lecture, coming to treat the subject as an important ethical question and to support research. People also ask whether the theory would lead to drastic measures, such as killing animals to stop them from reproducing. Cunha replies that, even for those who look only at consequences, there are good reasons for a general rule against killing: killing reinforces the idea that animals are disposable and confuses help with environmental management, with worse effects in the long run (N17, N11).',
    'There is an internal disagreement. Classical utilitarians add up well-being and suffering; suffering-focused authors, such as Brian Tomasik and Magnus Vinding, give priority to reducing it. The difference matters here, because the balance of welfare in nature is uncertain (Groff and Ng, 2019) and may even be positive (Browning and Veit, 2023). Cunha argues that, even with a positive balance, there would still be an enormous number of lives going very badly, and that the reason to help those lives does not depend on the balance (N1).']),
 'contratualismo': ('The contract and wild animals', [
    'Even without including animals, no rational contractor would approve a total ban on intervening in nature, because intervening is necessary for their own survival. This alone shows that "do not interfere with nature" cannot be a principle of the contract (N9).',
    'Behind Rowlands\'s veil, which also hides species, the parties do not know whether they would be born human or as one of the animals living outside human control. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), argues that they would choose to help those in danger regardless of whether the harm has a human or a natural origin, and would reject limiting help to those who can reciprocate. Since wild animals are astronomically more numerous than humans, the chance of being born as one of them would weigh heavily in the choice (N6, N7).']),
 'deontologia': ('Rights and wild animals', [
    'Tom Regan argued that, toward wild animals, the duty is to "let them be": rights forbid harming them, but do not require protecting them from predators and disease. Gary Francione goes in the same direction by admitting only negative rights. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), challenges both. The charge of paternalism does not apply, because nothing suggests that animals prefer not to be helped: fleeing from someone who approaches shows fear of a threat, not refusal of help. And Regan\'s argument that wild animals have the capacity to escape dangers slides from the species, which does survive, to individuals, most of whom are young and do not survive (N8, N4).',
    'Other authors within the rights tradition defend positive duties. Henry Shue, in Basic Rights (1980), holds that the basic rights to security and subsistence also require protection and aid from others. Kyle Johannsen and Jeff McMahan, who accept deontological constraints, argue for helping wild animals. And Cunha notes that deontology limits the means of helping, such as the ban on killing, but does not support an absolute ban on intervening: the maxim "never intervene in nature" could not hold as a universal law, because no agent survives without intervening (N6, N13).',
    'There is a further tension. Those who reject helping out of respect for rights sometimes appeal to consequences, saying that preventing predation would cause an explosion of prey and more deaths from starvation. But a theory that denies that consequences decide what is right cannot turn to them only in this case. The disagreement also runs the other way: Cunha finds it absurd to see exploitation as wrong in itself, regardless of how it affects animals, and abolitionists and followers of Regan disagree with him on this point (N17).']),
 'libertarianismo': ('What about wild animals?', [
    'A theory of negative rights only says that we must not aggress, but it does not oblige anyone to come to the rescue. Rainer Ebert and Tibor Machan, in "Innocent Threats and the Moral Problem of Carnivorous Animals" (2012), discuss what a theory of animal rights implies in the face of predation and point, as one way out, to a version based on negative rights. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), replies that, even accepting only negative rights, helping wild animals would remain permissible and praiseworthy, and that, if what justifies negative rights is the interests of those who can be harmed, the same interests also justify some assistance. For a libertarian, that help can come from donations and voluntary action, without depending on the state (N6, N13).']),
 'igualitarismo': ('Wild animals: who is worse off', [
    'Wild animals are a central case for egalitarianism and prioritarianism. Most animals are born into species that have hundreds, thousands or millions of offspring, and almost all of them die shortly after birth, from hunger, cold, disease or predation. If the reason to help grows stronger the worse off someone is, these lives are among those that most call for help (N1).',
    'The idea that disadvantages no one chose are unjust, discussed by authors such as Nils Holtug and Kasper Lippert-Rasmussen, also answers the image of a "fair nature" in which every animal has weapons to escape. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), notes that having similar capacities does not guarantee the same luck, and that predators pick precisely the weakest (N4).',
    'He also replies to those who say that welfare in nature may have a positive balance, as Groff and Ng (2019) and Browning and Veit (2023) argue. For egalitarian, prioritarian and sufficientarian theories, a positive aggregate balance does not cancel the reasons to help those who are very badly off, just as the wealth of some does not lessen the weight of others\' hunger.']),
 'cuidado': ('Care and wild animals', [
    'There is a version of care ethics that limits duties to those close to us and is used against helping wild animals. Grace Clement, in "The Ethic of Care and the Problem of Wild Animals" (2003), applies care against intervention in nature, and Clare Palmer, in Animal Ethics in Context (2010), defends a "laissez-faire" intuition toward wild animals: there would generally be no duty to assist them, because we do not have with them the relationship we have with domesticated animals (N7).',
    'Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), and Catia Faria, in "Disentangling Obligations of Assistance" (2015), reply that relationships alone do not decide who deserves help. Faria imagines three options: pressing a button that immunizes all humans against cancer, pressing another that immunizes only the people close to us, or pressing neither. Almost no one finds choosing the second acceptable. And those who use the lack of a relationship to avoid helping wild animals lose the argument against exploitation, because we have no relationship with the farmed animals we buy either (K2).']),
 'virtudes': ('Wild animals: Hursthouse\'s objection', [
    'Rosalind Hursthouse, in "Virtue Ethics and the Treatment of Animals" (2011), acknowledges that suffering usually predominates in the lives of wild animals, but rejects intervening to help them: it would destroy the characteristic forms of life of each species, it would come from applying general principles, which virtue ethics distrusts, and it would impose anthropocentric values.',
    'Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), replies that the rule "reject general principles" is itself a general principle, and that Hursthouse\'s conclusion also rests on one: preserving characteristic forms of life, however bad they are for those who live them. No one applies that principle to humans: we treat diseases that are also part of our species\' life. He adds that "never intervene in nature" could not be a virtue, because it would prevent cultivating any other, and that a compassionate person would not stay indifferent to immense suffering just because they did not cause it (N6, N8).']),
 'capacidades': ('Wild animals and flourishing', [
    'Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), highlights a point on which Nussbaum agrees with those who defend helping: in Frontiers of Justice, she separates the predator\'s blame from the question of preventing harm to the prey. The lion is not to blame for anything, but that does not settle whether we should protect the gazelle, as Steve Sapontzis and Tyler Cowen also observed (F4).',
    'He also points to a tension in the idea of flourishing according to one\'s own nature, defended in different versions by Thomas Hurka and by Jennifer Everett. If a species\' nature makes almost all of its young starve to death soon after birth, leaving them to it is not what is best for them; it is a reason to change those conditions, as we do with diseases that are also natural (N2, N8).']),
 'liberalismo': ('Zoopolis and wild animals', [
    'In Zoopolis, Sue Donaldson and Will Kymlicka treat communities of wild animals as sovereign, entitled to govern themselves without interference, which limits intervention to cases that do not threaten that autonomy. Alasdair Cochrane, in "Cosmozoopolis" (2013), and Oscar Horta, in "Zoopolis, Interventions and the State of Nature" (2013), push back. For Horta, the situation of wild animals resembles a humanitarian catastrophe. He, Catia Faria and Luciano Carlos Cunha describe most wild populations as "failed states", unable to secure the well-being of their members, since almost all of them die soon after birth. Faria goes further: if sovereignty is justified by the interests of individuals, it calls for "environmental improvement", that is, changing natural conditions when this improves the animals\' well-being (N8).',
    'There is also the question of paternalism, dear to liberalism. Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), notes that there is no sign that animals prefer not to be helped: when they flee from those trying to rescue them, they show fear of a threat, not refusal of help. And many kinds of help, such as water, food or vaccine baits, take away no one\'s liberty (N1).']),
 'comunitarismo': ('Community and wild animals', [
    'The territory criterion, on which domestic animals are "ours" and wild ones belong to another community, has a communitarian basis: J. Baird Callicott\'s mixed community, which gives domesticated animals the rights of members and denies wild ones even the right to life. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), notes that the criterion is applied only when the victims are not human: almost no one thinks that malaria should go untreated among peoples who live in the forest because they would belong to another "territory". And the historical debt to domesticated animals would give them, at most, priority, not a reason to exclude wild ones (N7).']),
 'altruismo-eficaz': ('Wild animals as a priority', [
    'By the criteria of scale, neglectedness and tractability, wild animal suffering is an extreme case. Brian Tomasik\'s estimates, which he himself treats as very uncertain, put the number of terrestrial arthropods alive at any one time somewhere between a hundred quadrillion and ten quintillion, plus tens to hundreds of trillions of fish, and almost no resources are devoted to them. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), argues that, even giving each human ten times more weight, the scale would require investing more in animals.',
    'Tractability is the most debated point. Cunha examines the efficiency objections: that acting would only be worthwhile on a large scale, that the effect would be short-term, that it would be "mopping up while the tap is running" because the animals breed again, and that it would take resources from other causes. He replies that sterilizing half of a population already prevents half of the births of young who would soon die, that research and outreach prepare larger actions, and that decisions should follow expected impact, not the worst-case scenario. He also warns against the proportion bias: judging a cause by the percentage of the problem that can be solved, rather than by the amount of harm prevented. But he admits that cost-effectiveness comparisons between this cause and others are lacking (N12, N13).']),
 'lei-natural': ('Intention, side effects and wild animals', [
    'The doctrine of double effect, which comes from the Thomist tradition, distinguishes the harm one causes on purpose from the harm one foresees as a side effect. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), discusses a similar idea, which he calls the intention requirement: we would only answer for the harm we intend to cause, not for the suffering we let happen. He replies with an example: someone who lets a child drown so as not to interrupt a television program did not intend the child\'s death, and is not innocent for that. The distinction may limit the means of helping, such as the ban on killing one animal to save others, but it does not show that there is no reason to help (N6).']),
 'religiao': ('Nature as the work of a good creator', [
    'A common objection says that nature does not err, or that the natural order was made by a creator and should not be corrected. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), replies that, even granting a creator, it does not follow that everything that happens in nature is good: one would have to show that this creator is good and wants exactly this outcome. He invites us to imagine someone malicious who wanted to maximize suffering: they would create beings with so many offspring that almost all starved soon after birth, and others that had to eat their prey still alive. The result would look a lot like the nature we know. For religious traditions, this is part of the problem of evil, not a reason to sit back: they already accept curing diseases and rescuing victims of floods and droughts, and the mercy they teach does not depend on the suffering having been caused by humans (N2).']),
 'relativismo': ('Is suffering just a preference?', [
    'A skeptical variant says that finding suffering bad is just a subjective preference, and that we have no way of knowing whether it really is bad. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), replies that, in this case, the judgment is about the experience itself, not about an object in the outside world: to suffer is, by definition, to live something as negative, and an experience that was not negative would not be suffering. This holds for whoever lives it, human or not, and whether it comes from a person or a disease. Those who accept this point for themselves have no reason to deny it to wild animals (N2).']),
 'egoismo-objetivismo': ('Egoism and the prey', [
    'Egoism appears indirectly in the debate on predation. It is sometimes said that the prey may defend itself, but that no one else may defend it, because each would only have the right to protect themselves. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), notes that this assumes an obligatory egoism, on which it would be wrong to care about others, something not even ethical egoists usually defend: the more common version says it is permissible to care only about oneself, not that it is forbidden to care about others. And, as the philosopher Stuart Rachels argues, the reason I have to end my pain comes from the pain being bad, not from its being mine; the same reason holds for anyone else\'s pain (N17, N4).']),
 'humanismo': ('The human test', [
    'One tool that runs through the debate on wild animals is the human test: asking what we would say if the victims were human. If human children were born by the thousands for almost all of them to starve or be eaten alive soon after, no one would say this is the natural order and that we must not interfere; and if humans were the usual prey of a predator, no one would hesitate to protect them. Luciano Carlos Cunha, in Razões para ajudar (2022), uses this test throughout the book, and it is a direct application of the reason and impartiality that humanism defends (N2, N17).',
    'The test also exposes an irony. By the usual definition of an invasive species, one that is not native to a place and threatens environmental goals, humans would be an invasive species almost everywhere on the planet. Almost no one concludes that it would be acceptable to kill them to protect an ecosystem, which shows that the criterion is applied only to other animals (A10, H19).']),
}
